28-year-old chef found dead in San Francisco mall
A 28-year-old chef who recently moved to San Francisco was found dead in the stairweak of a local shopping centre.
The victim's brother testified that he can't imagine anyone who would want to harm him, "Finally he was going uphill again."
The corpse, found Wednesday morning at the Westfield Mall, was identified as 28-year-old Frank Galicia of San Francisco, the forensic medical department in San Francisco said.
The San Francisco Police Department said the death has been classified as murder and the investigation is ongoing.
The victim's brother, Louis Galicia, told AB's KGO broadcaster in San Francisco that Frank, who formerly worked as a chef in Bosboston, grabbed his dream job as a chef at the Sons & Daughters restaurant in San Francisco six months ago.
A spokesperson for the Sons & Daughters said they were "shocked and devastated" over his death.
"We are a small team that works like a close family and we're going to miss him sorely," the spokesman continued.
Our thoughts and condolences are with Frank's family and friends during this difficult time.
Louis Galicia stated that Frank initially lived in hostels, but that "things finally went uphill for him."
"He found an apartment, he had a girlfriend," Louis Garcia KGO shared.
Louis Galicia said he couldn't envision anyone wanting to harm his younger brother.
He was a friendly person with a big heart.
His way of connecting with his family has always been to cook something for us, to prepare us for dinner," Louis Galicia said.
He never wanted to participate in any kind of altercation.
He was the brother who swam with the stream.
"With everything going wrong in the world, he was this unpolished diamond that shone brightly every day," he said.
Everyone who has information about the case is asked to call the SFPD’s tip-off phone at 415-575-4444.
Jennifer Aniston: I'm always put in drawers
Jennifer Aniston doesn't always have to be perfect or successful.
The Hollywood star has made this unequivocally clear in an interview.
"I'm always put in drawers or get labels glued up from the outside, which usually have little to do with me and reality."
“I don’t have a desire to always be perfect and successful.”
"Honestly, these things are rather sniffy for me."
Jennifer Aniston (47) can only laugh at the fact that she is considered "Miss Perfect," as she explains in the current "Cosmopolitan."
The actress prefers to spend her time at home, in front of the TV with a loose bun and Mexican food, according to the magazine.
“We women don’t have to be married or have children to be perfect,” the Hollywood star said.
In her new film "Mother's Day - love is not a children's game," Aniston is seen as a single mother of two sons.
The film will take place with us from August 25.
Golfer Langer receives the sports pyramid
His experiences on the horse are manageable.
At the end of a golf tournament in South South Africa, Bernhard Langer and some colleagues sought a bit of variety with a ride on the beach.
For the 58-year-old, it was a first.
CHIO: "Golden sports pyramid" for Bernhard Langer
After a few minutes, the horses remembered their end of work, they chased home to the food troughs in a quick gallop.
One kilometre at that pace, I've been scared.
"It wasn't a good experience," Langer says.
And that's when the riding career ended again.
On Saturday Germany's best golfer of all time - 101 tournament victories - was back near horses.
He and his family were guided over the ALRV facility, on a horse he didn’t even swipe for photo purposes.
Langer was in Aachen for the first time and thus also with the CHIO.
The reason was quite convincing.
The German sports aid handed the golden sports pyramid to the man from Anhausen as part of a small gala.
The sympathetic athlete is not lacking in awards.
Bundesverdienstkreuz, Silver Laurel Leaf, Champions-Tour-Player of the year...
Even the British Queen ennobled him already.
Langer is the 18th winner of the sports pyramid.
In Aachen on Saturday he met the first prize winner Hans Günter Winkler.
"This award means a lot to me," says Langer, "because it is awarded not only for sporting success, but also for social commitment."
Langer has been promoting young people for decades.
And so he also passed on the prize money of 25,000 euros immediately.
With 15 other outstanding athletes, the two-time U.S. Masters winner was inducted into the "Hall of Fame of German Sport" on Saturday.
Among the winners are Hartwig Gauder and Harald Schmitt, gymnast Eberhard Gienger, fencers Cornelia Hanisch, Alexander Pusch and Arnd Schmitt, rower Peter-Michael Kolbe, bobsleigh driver Meinhard Nehmer, judoka Frank Wieneke, show jumper Alwin Schockemöhle and posthumously the handballer Erhard.
Many of these sports legends had come to Aachen on Saturday.
According to the official part, illustrious society migrated to the Springstadion, where the Lavazza Prize ran.
Bernhard Langer kept his distance from the large animals.
Clinton's and Trump's economic policy by comparison: It's all about the money
Investing in infrastructure and preventing the TTP trade agreement - that's what both Clinton and Trump want.
However, the two U.S. presidential applicants disagree on tax and energy policy as well as a fundamental question.
The differences in economic policy between Donald Trump and Hillary Clinton are based on a fundamental, rather philosophical difference.
The one about the role of the state.
Trump, if in doubt, wants to keep them rather small and warns against how the state can harm the economy - with taxes and regulations.
Clinton wants to tax the economy to death and regulate to death, he says.
He himself, on the other hand, would be quite different, Trump claims.
He will massively reduce regulations, and he is campaigning for far-reaching tax reform with lower tax rates - for private individuals and for companies.
Clinton, on the other hand, says: The state has certain tasks to fulfill, it has disadvantaged opportunities to open and needs money for it.
That's why taxes for the rich would be increased, she says.
Interestingly, both are very close when it comes to infrastructure.
It has to be invested in them, Trump and Clinton say almost identically.
The democratic candidate even announces the largest investment in new jobs since the Second World War.
State investment programs in the infrastructure are classic democratic politics and are strictly rejected, for example, by the state-critical teaparty wing of the Republicans.
So Trump could still face party-internal discussions.
The differences in energy and climate policy are more apparent.
Trump wants to build coal-fired power plants and restart coal production in the U.S.
Oil production should also be possible in previously protected areas, he wants to terminate the Paris climate protection agreement.
Clinton, on the other hand, wants to explicitly expand renewable energies with her investment programme and demands: "A country will become the superpower of clean energies and create millions of jobs."
“It’s going to be either China, Germany or America—I want us.”
But not only in terms of infrastructure - even with international trade agreements, the two competitors aren't too far apart.
TPP, the trans-Pacific trade deal, both don't want to sign.
At the same time, however, both do not want to isolate the U.S. from international trade - but simply to better protect American jobs.
They should then also be paid better, for example Clinton demands.
The current minimum wage of US$7.25 is a starvation wage.
She wants to raise him to 15 dollars an hour.
What Trump wants to do on this issue isn't entirely clear: once he says: the minimum wage has to go up, then he says he shouldn't be changed.
But the great discussion of the coming months is likely to develop around the tax policy of the two camps.
On the one hand, Trump, who wants to relieve the economy and the citizens to boost growth.
His criticians tell him that the budget deficit would only increase.
On the other side, Clinton, who at least wants to demand from the right that they pay more taxes, and whose critics say: more taxes are not the solution, either.
Escape: "Every refugee woman has experience with sexual violence"
Sexual violence is almost part of everyday life for women fleeing alone.
When they arrive in Germany, they are often traumatized.
A house offers refuge.
There are four women sitting in a large kitchen.
Everyone has a baby who is only one year old.
One has put her little son in his arms.
He looks curiously into the room with big eyes.
His mother is staring at the table top.
Until she realizes she's being watched.
She smiles briefly and kisses the little one.
The scene looks normal.
Until you're talking to Katrin Bahr.
She is area managing director of Condrobs, a carrier for social assistance in Bavaria, and deals, among other things, with the topic of women on the run.
The four women in the kitchen are refugees.
They live in the north of Munich in one of the few decentralized shelters for refugee women and their children in Bavaria.
In January, Condrobs opened the facility together with Pro Familia and Münchner Frauenhilfe.
It has recently been officially opened.
60 people live there - about 40 women and 20 children.
Men don't exist - even as visitors.
The exception is a security guard at the door.
"It's not exaggerated to say: every woman who arrives here has had experiences with sexual violence on her escape," Bahr says.
"Men are fleeing because of wars because they are prosecuted politically because they face torture or death or because their families send them to Europe as bearers of hope," she recounts.
For women, other reasons for escaping predominate: "Many secretly steal from it because they don't see another way out."
In their homeland, they have had experience with sexual violence, forced marriages, honor killings, slavery or forced prostitution.
That'll drive them away.
On the run, this continues.
"The fact that tugboats, for example, demand a sexual act for a bottle of water is not uncommon," Bahr says.
And the women are alone, they aren't protected by their families.
Anyone who thinks this will end as soon as they arrive in Germany is mistaken, says Bahr.
Here, too, single women in refugee shelters would often be considered inferior and abused at worst.
As a result, about 90 percent of the women who live in accommodation in the north of Munich are traumatized, she estimates.
"Some of the children certainly didn't arise voluntarily."
Many women are so used to they say nothing.
“Or they don’t dare to speak openly about what they’ve experienced, because the shame is too great.”
Since they often don’t know how to help each other, it needs more accommodation just for them, Bahr demands.
In Bavaria, the seven district governments are responsible for the accommodation of refugees.
Asked if they knew of a similar project as in Munich in their area of responsibility, some answer no.
In the Upper Palatinate and Upper Franconia, for example, there is no such thing in shelters of the governments.
Even in Swabia, only refugee women are not accommodated separately, says Karl-Heinz Meyer, press spokesman for the government.
In Upper Bavaria, Lower and Middle Franconia there are floors in some communal shelters or own houses only for single women and their children.
The exception is Lower Bavaria, where there are two central accommodations exclusively for women.
The Committee on Women's Rights and Gender Equality in the European Parliament also calls for women to be accommodated separately by men.
According to a study published for the committee, "it is very important that men and women are accommodated separately."
“If privacy is lacking or men and women are accommodated together, there may be – more continuous – violence against women.”
In particular, the washrooms should be separate and easily accessible for women, the study says.
How much the women feel this need for protection can be seen in the crowd that exists on the house in Munich.
"Since it's been getting around that it's been around, we've had a long waiting list," Bahr says.
One could easily open a second house.
Residential doctors strike: Demands for new industrial action
Deputies of the assistant physicians have called on their union to approve new industrial action in their disputes regarding a new contract.
The Junior Doctors Committee (JDC) of the British Medical Association (BMA) has demanded its entire advice to approve more industrial action from the beginning of September.
The JDC testified that ministers had failed to respond to concerns about the treaty.
Assistant physicians and medical students voted in July on the rejection of a contract agreed with the BMA.
It was rejected by 58% of its members who voted in the elections.
In a letter to members posted on Twitter on Thursday night, JDC leader Ellen McCourt said the government would have remained "persistently calm" about problems, causing the contract to be rejected.
She said, "In light of this, the JDC board has agreed to completely reject the proposed new contract and requested official renegotiations on all your concerns."
In response to the government's silence, the JDC board today made a formal request for a special meeting of the BMA Council to approve an ongoing programme of escalating industrial action that begins at the beginning of September.
The disputes caused residents to strike six times this year, the first overall failures in the history of the NHS.
The head of BMA assistant physician Dr. Johann Malawana resigned after the election to reject the agreed contractual conditions, which had been recommended by the BMA.
He informed BMA members during the meetings, which took place even before the vote by 54,000 assistant physicians and medical students, that the contract was beneficial and should be accepted.
After the vote, Health Secretary Jeremy Hunt said the contract for doctors in England would be forcibly introduced.
Russia and Turkey: An "alliance of outsiders"?
It was a gesture that ended a crisis.
The leaders of Russia and Turkey met on Tuesday to give their hands and announce an official end to the eight-month-long war of words and economic sanctions.
But when Vladimir Putin welcomes his Turkish opponent in the gilded hall of the St. Petersburg Palace, I can't deny myself the impression that Ankara wants this reconciliation the most.
There's been the handshake, yeah.
But Putin's smile seemed narrow-lipped and he barely radiated warmth, even for his restrained circumstances.
Recep Tayyip Erdogan, on the other hand, repeatedly spoke of his "dear friend" Putin - according to a report five times.
I couldn't count it anymore.
He also promised that relations with Russia would not only return to their old levels before the crisis, but will even improve.
The next day, a newspaper described that Erdogan behaved as if nothing bad had ever happened.
His enthusiasm seemed to suggest the opposite.
But a sustained coolness radiated by Putin showed that Russia's head of state hadn't forgotten anything.
Rather, the cause of the crisis was the first thing he mentioned in his introductory words: the downing of a Russian warplane on the Syrian border by Turkey.
Moscow's reaction at the time was furious.
Putin divided, accusing Ankara of falling in Russia's backs.
The offence was even greater because it was committed by a supposed friend.
Rebuilding confidence will become difficult, if not impossible.
Public opinion in Russia has changed since November.
For months, the state-controlled media carried out a violent, unrestricted attack on Ankara.
All of a sudden it seemed like the Turks were to blame for everything.
Most serious were the accusations from senior officials that Erdogan's own family had benefited from the illegal oil trade in areas of Syria controlled by the so-called Islamic State.
He vehemently disputed this.
But in St.Petersburg the official message was that it was time to look forward.
After all, this meeting took place only because Putin received the apology he wanted to hear from President Erdogan.
Russia was able to account for some kind of victory.
For Ankara, the benefits of a ceasefire are clearly obvious.
Erdogan primarily needs all the friends he can get, after he was almost relieved of office last month in a failed coup.
Repeated terrorist attacks on Turkey have also rocked him.
But there are also economic motives.
Russian sanctions have hit Turkey hard - in particular the ban on charter flights, which usually bring several million Russian tourists to the Turkish coast every year.
The number has declined by almost 90%.
In Russia, tour operators and charter companies will be relieved when flights are resumed.
You are counting on a late-season onslaught to the Mediterranean.
This week, state television predicted cheaper fruit and vegetables once Turkish agricultural imports are allowed again.
A report in Vedemosti put it this way: "Tourists there long, tomatoes here long."
But the visit had additional political value for Moscow.
Ankara is angry with the West, as it sees its reaction to the attempted seizure of power as a weak response.
In addition, there is the ongoing resentment about the snail's pace of EU accession negotiations and Putin - which is eager to take advantage of the cool mood and reduce Turkey's relationship with the West.
The Russian head of state certainly won a bonus in Ankara for demanding support for the elected authorities after the attempted coup.
This is a matter of course for Moscow, which itself has a deep-seated fear of regime changes.
The summit meeting in this glamorous palace by the sea made it possible for Russia and Turkey to represent, as an analyst once described to me, an "alliance of outsiders": two lands that feel rejected by the West and feel badly treated and now ally.
Nevertheless, despite public reconciliation, there are still great differences between the two.
The biggest difference is Syria, where Moscow recently gave itself the role as a peacemaker, but where Russia and Turkey support opposing sides.
It could be indicative that, after almost three hours of initial talks, both presidents testified at the press conference that they hadn't even streaked the issue.
Turkey's president intentionally avoided responding to a question about their differences, while Putin decided to emphasize them.
There is no clear consensus on where a common denominator can be found on Syria.
But after months of open hostilities - and given the potential for a complete disaster when NATO member Turkey shot down the Russian fighter jet - it's certainly better that the two heads of state at least talk to each other again.
Royal Bank of Scotland no longer available to customers outside Scotland
According to the bank's chief executive, the RBS brand will only be reduced to a back-office role.
Royal Bank of Scotland will no longer be available to customers outside Scotland.
Ross McEwan told BBC Scotland that the RBS brand was linked to the bank's global ambitions.
The bank has since distanced itself when it nearly collapsed and had to be bailed out eight years ago.
During this period, brand strategists have used "RBS" to protect other end-user financial brands.
She supported the international sport with millions of pounds in sponsorship, from Six Nations rugby to Wimbledon victor Andy Murray.
But now she has decided to focus on more national brands.
Royal Bank of Scotland will only be available to customers in Scotland but will not be initialised.
In England and Wales, all RSB references, decentralised headquarters and stock exchange listing will move to NatWest.
Ulster Bank brand is already being used for customers in the Republic of Ireland and Northern Ireland.
There are other, smaller brands for retail banking that will gain more importance - Coutts, Adam & Co, Drummond and Holt's Military Bank.
McEwan was interviewed during a sightseeing tour by customers and staff in Inverness-shire.
He told BBC Scotland: "The RSB brand will become our investor brand as well as the brand under which our employees will be employed as we are now becoming more a bank of brands."
When the bank itself became a global brand, RBS became a global brand.
I'm saying now that we're not pursuing global goals, but local goals.
Each of these brands will stand for something completely different in its own communities and our employees will work with customers under these brands.
RBS has already testified that it will no longer continue it and that it will increase the perceptiveness of the various brands in its sports sponsorship.
"It's time for us to develop into the bank of the brands, because how do we're focusing inside (we've asked) to make this a better bank for customers?" the chief executive said.
It would have been very cynical three years ago if we'd said we're going to be a great bank for our customers and launched those brands.
But with our work, the focus on customer needs and not our own, I think you'll see a lot of change.
We can launch these brands again, so I think the time has come.
Frankie Dettori achieves his 3,000th profit at Newmarket on Predilection
Dettori sprays champagne after a double at Newmarket, which earns him 3,000th profit
Jockey Frankie Dettori secured his 3,000th UK profit at Newmarket on Friday.
He relied on predilection, which is coached by John Gosden, on his local racetrack with his second win of the night a milestone.
The 45-year-old Italian is the sixth jockey to reach the milestone in the flat race.
He succeeds Sir Gordon Richards, Doug Smith, Lester Piggott, Pat Eddry and Willie Carson.
Three-time winner Dettori, who won on Ghayyar before, said: "It's special."
I'm very emotional because my family is here too
I'm very happy that it's happened at Newmarket because I landed here 30 years ago.
It's not a big meeting, it's not Royal Ascot - it's a mundane Friday night with 20,000 people.
I'm relieved and very modest.
Dettori picks up the jubilation as he picks up victory on predilection
Two jockeys have reached the milestone in the obstacle race - Jockey Richard Johnson and the retired 20-time victor Sir Anthony McCoy, who achieved more than 4,300 victories in a record-breaking career.
Lester Piggott, the nine-time derby winner had 4,493 career victories
Frankie is one of the best jockey of modernity and it's no surprise he's reached this milestone that few have reached before him.
He has style and is strong, but it's his tactical intellect that distinguishes him from the others in the weight control room.
He's a fantastic ambassador for races, his enthusiasm and charisma has been thrilling fans for this sport for years.
I told Frankie at Epsom a few weeks ago that he would have already won 6,000 if he wasn't just a weekend and major tournament jockey!
Frankie has already ridden in GB for almost 30 years, which is great.
He has a vibrant personality, which is good for a British race, but beyond that he's an unbelievable jockey.
Frankie Dettori celebrated the milestone with a typical Flying Dismount
From Flying Finishes to Flying Dismounts, Frankie Dettori has been the figurehead of the British flat race for two decades.
It was nerve-wracking.
A whole series of majoring Tournament wins, including his famous "Glorious Seven" in Ascot in 1996, but he also survived a plane crash and received a drug ban.
Three years ago he initially struggled when he returned from his six-month suspension, but he showed an impressive character strength to defy his doubters and return to his best form.
And he's far from over - Dettori once said that he wants to continue in competitions up to the age of at least 50th.
Russia's Putin fires chief of staff Sergei Ivanov
Russian President Vladimir Putin has unexpectedly sacked his chief of staff Sergei Ivanov.
Ivanov was part of Putin's closest circle of trust for many years.
The 63-year-old has been appointed special representative for environmental and transport matters.
According to the Kremlin, it reads that Putin, without giving reasons, "ordered Ivanov's dismissal of his duties as chairman of the Russian presidential administration."
Anton Vaino, who has been Ivanov's deputy since 2012, has been named his successor.
Vaino, 44, is a former diplomat.
Born in 1972 in the Estonian capital Tallinn, he graduated at the prestigious Moscow State Institute of International Relations (MGIMO) and worked at the embassy in Tokyo.
On the Kremlin's website it says (in Russian) that he was later chief of protocol and chief of staff of government personnel.
About his appointment, he said to Putin: "Thank you for your trust."
I think it is the government's most important task to support your work as head of state with regard to draft laws and checks on the implementation of your instructions.
Putin told the Russian TV broadcaster on Friday that Ivanov had asked to leave his post and recommended Vaino as his successor.
Convince the smiling faces?
This move has made Moscow a crime-revoked.
Sergei Ivanov has long been one of Vladimir Putin's closest confidants and, like him, works for the Soviet secret secriety, the KGB.
As chief of staff, he was one of the most powerful men in the country.
At a meeting with President Putin, which aired on television, both men claimed that the chief of staff was stepping down at his own request.
But despite the smile for the cameras, few are convinced here - especially right now, just before the parliamentary elections.
Is this the consequence of a kind of power struggle?
No one knows that at the moment.
But the official claim - that a man who was once touted as a potential president suddenly wants to lead Russia's environmental policies - encountered great skepticism.
In his remarks to Putin, quoted on the Kremlin's website, Ivanov said "it's true that I asked you in a conversation in early 2012 to entrust me this very complicated item - you could almost say - arduous posts for four years.
As it turns out, I have been chairman of the Russian presidential administration for four years and eight months.
Ivanov took the post in December 2011.
He had previously been deputy prime minister and secretary of defense.
He is a member of the Russian Security Council and, like Putin, is a former member of the KGB state security service.
In the late 1990s, when Putin was chairman of the Federal Security Service (FSB), which replaced the KGB, Ivanov was appointed his deputy.
When Putin came to power, he named Ivanov as one of five people he trusted most.
It was once assumed that Ivanov could become president of Russia after Putin's second tenure, as Putin's third term would have violated the constitution.
But this post was taken by another, close-knocked ally Putin, Dmitry Medvedev.
Putin became prime minister before becoming president again three-and-a-half years later.
Criminal Mind's star Thomas Gibson fired after beating producers
Gibson was involved in a physical altercation on the set of the series in Los Angeles last month.
ABC Studios and CBS Television Studios, which produce the series, announced in a joint statement its termination.
Gibson, who played special agent Aaron Hotchner, publicly apologized on Thursday prior to announcing his termination.
On set there were artistic differences and a disagreement.
I regret the incident," he said in a statement.
Details of how the character Gibsons from the series, which follows a team ofrom FBI agents, will probably be released at a later date, according to ABC Studios and CBS Television Studios.
The 54-year-old, who previously starred on sitcom Dharma & Greg, has been there since the first season of the series in 2005.
"I love Criminal Minds and I've been putting all my lifeblood in the last twelve years," Gibson said in a statement released on Friday.
I'd hoped to be there until the end, but that won't be possible anymore.
I’d love to thank the writers, producers, actors, our wonderful crew and, above all, the best fans you could wish for for a series.
The planned broadcast of the twelfth season is scheduled for September 28.
Mysteries about fish that ended up in garden in Banff
A Banff resident called for help in identifying the fish that appeared in his garden in Banff.
Kevin Bain thinks that the 75 pies could be sand eels spewered out by a fountain.
Mr Bain, who lives about 500 meters from the sea, posted on Periscope a video of the spectacle in the hope that anyone could shed some light on the appearance of the two-inch-long fish.
Queensferry Crossing: Bridge now connected to Fife
The £1.35bn project is due to be completed by May 2017
Engineers have connected the north deck of Queensferry Crossing with the viaduct - which means the bridge is now connected to Fife.
It is the first of four closures of the "deck blowers" approaching completion around each of the bridge's three towers.
Fife's bridge section is now 600 metres long and weighs 30,000 tonnes.
It has 10,000 tons of steel and 20,000 tons of concrete as well as 46 slewing ropes.
The £1.35bn project is due to be completed by May 2017.
Business Secretary Keith Brown paid a visit to the construction site today and was one of the first to go from the countryside to the bridge.
He said: "This is a historical and symbolic moment in the construction of Queensferry Crossing.
We are all witnesses to an engineering art of a truly epic measurement in this project with over 30,000 tons of concrete and steel used for this part of the bridge alone.
Despite the massive size and weight of the bridge, the completion of the closure between the viaduct and the bridging deck is a delicate activity that requires extremely precise tolerances for the fit.
In total, almost 79% of the total bridge decks are in place and the last section of the desk today in Rosyth gets a concrete casting for the deck, which means that the entire deck is ready to be lifted on the bridge to its place.
Michael Martin, project manager of Forth Crossing Bridge Constructors said: "The first closure represents a significant milestone in every bridge project.
In this fantastic project, the closure represents pioneering building and civil engineering.
After the installation of the last section of the road deck and its connection to the northern tower deck under construction, we then had to close the remaining gap on the north or land side.
This was achieved 700 millimeters to the south by pulling the northern feeder bridge.
This has been a massive and at the same time very delicate activity.
Massive, since the viaduct is 222 meters long, weighs about 6,000 tons and had to be pulled upwards at an incline of about 3%.
Delicate, since the tolerances that the team is working towards were tiny - on each side they were only a few millimeters.
Luckily, everything went very well.
Saving in Moelfre Bay: Three men 'have 'happiness yet to be alive'
Three men have "happiness yet to be alive" after being throwned off Anglesey by their fishing boat.
The Moelfre RNLI rescue station sounded the alarm on Friday at 13:20 BST after a crew watched the event in Moelfre Bay.
The three men struggled to swim to safety while their boat moved on by their own.
"We seen the boat around her and envisioned the worst," Moelfre Vince Jone's header said.
"Fortunately, the strong winds have already blown the boat out of their reach until we left to water," he added.
Two of the men were rescued by a local fishing boat, while the third man, aged 60, was found hanging from a buoy without a life jacket.
They all came aboard the coastal rescue boat and were taken back to the rescue station
Mr Jones said: "The three men who were thrown into the water are lucky unharmed and still alive.
The boat engine had an emergency stop line, but it detached from the person who steered the boat when the boat capsized.
Someone woke over these men today.
Seriously injured - man stabs his ex-girlfriend
A man has attacked and seriously injured his ex-girlfriend in Hamburg-Harburg with a knife.
According to the police yesterday, the 50-year-old got the key to the apartment to get his personal belongings.
Instead, the alcoholic perpetrator smashed his ex-girlfriend's clothes.
When she came into the apartment, he stabbed her twice.
The 44-year-old was injured on the hand and body.
Police were able to arrest the perpetrator.
While the 50-year-old destroyed his ex-girlfriend's clothes, he called his mother.
She then alerted his sister.
Together with her partner, the 45-year-old wanted to reassure her brother.
But she couldn't persuade him to leave the apartment.
That's why she walked out of the house with her significant other.
On the road, they met the man's ex-partner.
When the two women entered the apartment again, the 50-year-old shoved his sister to the side and attacked his ex-girlfriend.
Who builds Brunswick's cheap apartments?
The SPD and Greens think that investors should be made much more accountable in the future.
How do you respond to the bad news that Brunswick will lose almost 1300 cheap apartments in the next few years?
The SPD proposes an "action concept of housing" at the next council meeting.
Strife is programmed, however.
First and foremost is the question: Who should actually build the cheap apartments?
A regulation is missing so far.
SPD parliamentary group chairman Christoph Bratmann says: "We are in agreement with the Greens: If you want to build on urban land, you will have to designate 20 percent of all apartments as social housing in the future."
However, the cheap apartments can also be created in stock.
However, according to Bratmann, the big point of contention in the city hall is: "How do you deal with investors who want to build on private land?"
"We want investors to build at least 10 percent on such land, but preferably 20 percent of apartments as social housing."
A new urban housing subsidy programme, which will cost millions and has yet to be funded, is intended to provide incentives.
The problem, Bratmann said, "there is currently no agreement between the political groups."
The CDU wants investors to make as few regulations as possible.
The left still want tough conditions for investors.
Regardless of this, according to Bratmann, a worsening of the situation in the housing market.
If the action concept is implemented, this will provide for additional tasks in the construction administration.
More personnel are needed.
It's unclear whether we're going to get the professionals.
But the program is not yet complete: because the approximately 1260 new apartments, which should be built anyway.
But how does one compensate for the loss of an additional 1300 cheap apartments, whose subsidy will expire in the next few years?
By 2020 alone, there will be 500 apartments.
In order to prevent this from happening, according to planning policy spokeswoman Nicole Palm, expiring ties should now be purchased by the city.
