The £2-per-day drug, which can lower heart attack risk
Thousands of heart attack patients will benefit from a £2-per-day drug after it has been recommended by the authorities to administer more people for an extended period of time.
The anticoagulant drug Ticagrelor reduces the risk of repeated heart attacks for people with heart disease.
The drug is given as early as 12 months after a heart attack, reducing the risk of a stroke or another heart attack.
NHCE's regulator NICE has recommended a four-year-long intake to further reduce the risk of cardiovascular problems.
NHCE's regulator NICE has recommended a four-year-long intake to further reduce the risk of cardiovascular problems.
Around 140,000 people suffer a heart attack every year and a quarter of them suffer another heart attack or stroke.
Heart attacks and strokes are caused by accumulations of fatty material in the arterial walls that form coverings.
If the torment breaks apart, it can cause a blood clot clogging the blood flow to the heart, causing a heart attack.
When the blood clot dissolves, it can flow through the bloodstream and clog the blood flow to the brain, causing a stroke.
Individuals who have already had a heart attack are at a higher risk of having another.
Ticagrelor, which is manufactured by the British company AstraZeneca and distributed under the trade name Brilique, reduces this risk by making the formation of blood clots more unlikely.
NICE's instruction draft, published today, recommends a 12-month intake of 90mg ticagrelor, followed by 60mg with a twice-daily intake of aspirin for the next three years.
Professor Carole Longson, director of the NICE health technology evaluation centre said: "Despite the availability of secondary prevention, a quarter of all people who have suffered a heart attack have another heart attack or stroke - often with disastrous consequences."
Fears of another heart attack can have significant negative effects on a person’s quality of life.
Experience has shown that Ticagrelor, combined with aspirin, is effective in reducing further heart attacks and strokes in people who have already had a heart attack.
Through a preliminary recommendation from Ticagrelor, we are pleased that we are able to extend available treatment options to thousands of people who may benefit.
The information on the efficacy and safety of Ticagrelor - especially the risk of bleeding - is limited to a period of up to three years. The instruction design does not recommend treatment that goes beyond this period.
