In 2021, the state election will not take place again directly at the end of the summer holidays, the Schwerin parliament decided at the beginning of the year.
Because it doesn't get stupider than before: summer holidays, heat and state election campaign - that bites.
The locals to be persuaded by election campaigners are on holiday.
The vast majority of holidaymakers who are in the country, on Usedom, Rügen or Hiddensee, are no locals, so they are not allowed to vote the state parliament.
In addition, there are practical problems: During the holidays, helpers who hang posters, inflate balloons, distribute flyers and in the end count votes, are only painstaking to find.
Until September 4, 2016, election and last holiday, Mecklenburg-Vorpommern once again has to endure an election campaign just past the people.
The hot phase, if you can say that at all, has just begun.
And it's also about something: Prime Minister Erwin Sellering, leader of an SPD/CDU coalition, must fear for his office.
The SPD, the strongest party since 1998, is now clearly behind the CDU in polls.
It becomes interesting to see how strongly newcomer AfD performs and what is left in the end suitable for coalitions.
Sellering, 66 years old and head of government since 2008, makes an almost inconspicuous-looking election campaign, apart from his big posters.
He travels through the country, distributes roses and chats a little with the people, just as his legendary wordless predecessor Harald Ringstorff did.
Other way, Seelering has gotten cold feet and blames Chancellor Merkel about the AfD's blooming.
Her refugee policy, her "We can do this", has accelerated the rise of the right-wing populists, which will probably cost him the beautiful office now.
The CDU with its top candidate, Interior Minister Lorenz Caffier, has already witnessed what it's like to campaign at the wrong time in the right place.
She has twice invited Chancellor Angela Merkel to major rallies; Merkel's federal constituency is located in the northeast.
But the target group was not right, there were more North Rhine-Westphalia, Berliners and Saxons there than Mecklenburgers and Vorpommern.
"There was probably the majority from other federal states," complained Vincent Kokert, secretary general of the CDU in Mecklenburg-Vorpommern, recently in the NDR.
But it doesn't hurt anyone from other corners of Germany to listen to Angela Merkel.
Otherwise, the Northeast CDU largely dispenses with the usual campaign stalls on marketplaces and in front of shopping centers with umbrellas and balloons.
According to Kokert, this only plays a "subordinate role".
The Union has also noticed that it is crumbling away in the countryside, that it has to put a lot of effort into finding a connection with the people again.
It is like the left, the SPD, the Greens: hardly present in the expanses of the country.
However, the advice of the CDU election campaigner Kokert to his colleagues is also self-exploratory: "You have to go where it hurts, namely where the people are: village festivals, golden weddings, every birthday party must be used".
Because a lot is at stake, Sellering has now called his SPD for a particularly committed election campaign.
In polls, the Social Democrats were last with 22 percent behind partner CDU (25) and just ahead of the AfD (19).
In the 2011 election, the SPD had clearly won with 35.6 percent ahead of the CDU (22).
The Greens, Kleinpartei with seven percent in the polls, are likely to make the leap into the state parliament.
They make a modern virtue out ofrom holiday distress and move their election campaign largely to the Internet.
"Online election campaign," the country's chairwoman Claudia Müller calls it.
Other way, green campaigners are embarking on a search for traces of local voters among holidaymakers.
Silke Gajek, the top candidate, curves through the Mecklenburg lakes with a houseboat and finds it still beautiful.
Her impression: "Election leave in Mecklenburg-Vorpommern is not a punishment".
B27 refurbishment: Hospitalities suffer from lockdown
There is nothing to complain about the speed with which the B 27 is being refurbished.
For those responsible for the construction work, which starts at 5.30 a.m. and ends around 8 p.m., there is praise everywhere.
"They've done everything pretty jagged," says Elvira Tetzlaff of the jet gas station.
That's a super team.
There's no delay either.
At the beginning of the week, the construction site could be relocated to the south at half-time according to plan, by the end of October everything should be ready.
It is also commended that those responsible for the construction work, for example when it comes to the establishment of makeshift entrances.
Since the construction site migrated to the south on Monday and the closure of Aspergstraße in Ofterdingen has also been lifted and thus the connection to Rottenburg has again existed, the traffic situation inside the village has relaxed.
Citizens can now use the B 27 on both sides again from Ofterdingen.
Evasion traffic through the place seems to be declining.
"There were sometimes considerable complaints," says the deputy head of office Florian King, who is responsible for the traffic management of the refurbishment.
The residents of Bachsatzstraße, Steinlachstraße, Hafnerstraße and Weiherrain were struggling with more traffic noise, because many sneaky roads searched and trusted their satnav rather than the signage.
This meant that some trucks frayed in residential areas.
