"There have been considerable transgressions," says King, who once made himself aware of the location.
There have already been two to three per minute flashed.
The fastest was with 77 kilometers per hour in the tempo 30 zone on the way.
Since the construction site moved on, however, it has become quieter.
For the business people on the B 27, it is only a small consolation that the road is now open again for customers from Rottenburg.
You don’t like to look into the cards for businessmen.
But that the B 27 renovation has led to revenue slumps with them, makes everyone you ask look through.
However, some are more and some less affected.
If you have less to do with the "end customer", i.e. produce for other companies, the refurbishment has hardly any consequences for you.
Thomas Sulz also speaks of an "almost slight decline" at Autohaus Maier, but also admits that, of course, "the running clientele is missing".
The restaurants along the road are the hardest battered.
J. Albert Göhner, owner of the steakhouse, formerly Krone, finds clear words.
That's existential.
Not only him, but all restaurants "hit it hard."
Customers from Hechingen, Balingen and Rottweil were missing.
We have to be happy about every euro turnover.
He estimates the loss of sales at "well over 50 percent."
The remediation of the road "threw back its operation by two years."
He had to fire half of his employees.
If I had a company with 4000 employees and half had been dismissed, the policy would have reacted differently.
Instead, in response to a question from Göhner whether there could be a financial compensation, government President Jörg Schmidt replied with the indication that the restaurant otherwise benefited from the advantages of the situation on the B 27. Now it would also have to bear the disadvantages that result from the renovation.
Göhner knows from other counties where something like this has been handled more generously.
In all lawsuits, Göhner is sure that his restaurant will continue to exist.
After all, the family business has been located here since 1715, it refers to the long history.
He can understand that other regular guests are now stopping by less frequently because of the complicated traffic management.
If you can't drive here, it's nice somewhere else.
Göhner has no doubt that his regular clientele comes back after the end of the renovation work.
Despite the crises with BSE and avian flu, Göhner considers the impact of the B 27 construction work on his clientele to be the biggest crisis his restaurant has experienced in its long history.
Six months of construction, that's brutal.
Laws are constantly being screwed to laws in the name of counter-terror
After the recent attacks, Interior Minister de Maizière is now presenting a thick security package.
But what has toleration status to do with terror?
And what does the Coalition partner say about all of this?
Thomas de Maizière gives the reconciliator.
All of his proposals are "politically reasonable" for the coalition partner, says the Federal Minister of the Interior at the presentation of his new security package.
You'ren't overwhelming anyone.
Other, far-reaching points would initially be discussed within the parties.
I'm involved in that, too.
As interior minister, however, he was interested in results and an "achievable consensus" within the coalition.
De Maizière passed a little time after the recent attacks in Würzburg and Ansbach, before presenting his conclusions for security in the republic.
However, they are now all the more extensive.
Some things have already been known, proclaimed, targeted: that a new security authority should take care of the cracking of encrypted communication is not new.
Also not the fact that Germany has to implement several EU requirements on passenger data and gun law.
The call for more video surveillance and personnel for the security authorities also comes as no surprise.
In part, de Maizière is launching a second attempt for proposals that could not be made with the SPD so far: he wants to criminalize sympathy advertising for terrorist groups and jihadists with dual citizenship to unbutton the German passport.
"These will be difficult points for the SPD," admits the CDU man.
Nevertheless, he thinks this is reasonable and does not demand anything that has no chance with the coalition partner anyway.
De Maizière also presents a few new ideas.
They have it in part: He plans rapid procedures for deportations and more opportunities to imprison people before deportation.
Refugees who are specifically brought into the country should have their smartphones checked beforehand and disclose social media accounts for a security check.
In the case of tolerations for rejected asylum seekers, he wants to make some even worse than before - for example those who commit crimes or deliberately throw away their papers in order to disguise their identities.
Some of it is politically and legally sensitive.
Some things still seem impoverable and leave questions unanswered.
And on some points - in the unequal treatment of patients, for example - the question arises: What does this actually have to do with security and terror?
Yes, says de Maizière, it is important to differentiate between refugees and terror issues.
But it cannot be denied that the assassins of Ansbach and Würzburg were refugees.
In addition, many people were worried about security in view of the high number of refugees.
And things that deeply preoccupied the people could not be excluded even in election campaign times.
Internal security has come into enormous focus.
Only the attacks in the neighbourhood: Paris, Copenhagen, Brussels, Nice.
Then the attacks in Würzburg and Ansbach.
Uncertainty is great.
And yes, there are election campaigns.
Union and SPD are therefore increasingly positioning themselves on the issue of internal security.
In the past few days, it went high between the two parties.
First a nasty dispute over the equipment of the federal police, then about a security policy idea collection of some Union interior ministers, in which the heads of the department also demand a burqa ban and a departure from dual citizenship.
De Maizière strives for conciliatory tones here too.
He doesn't think anything of a departure from the double passport.
We pacified the discussion in Germany.
Reopening them doesn't make sense.
And with a burqa ban, he has constitutional concerns.
Whether his own package at all corners is constitutional and whether it is actually "reasonable" for the coalition partner has yet to become clear.
There's a lot in it that could cause strife.
But the SPD is also currently trying to distinguish itself on the issue of internal security.
Party leader Sigmar Gabriel initially cautiously signals a willingness to talk and says: "The SPD is ready to talk about everything that contributes to further increasing security."
Volkswagen is still getting through lightly.
Despite diesel scandals, delivery figures are rising worldwide.
But it’s not going well in all markets.
In the recall campaign, there are vortices around a term.
Volkswagen starts more subdued into the second half of the year in terms of deliveries.
Europe’s largest car manufacturer can continue to show an increase in the course of the year thanks to its many brands and some well-running markets in the course of the year.
But in July, the core VW brand weakened, especially in Western Europe, putting a strain on overall sales again, according to the company listed in the Dax.
With 449100 cars worldwide, the VW brand sold 1.8 percent fewer vehicles in July than in the same month last year.
In Western Europe alone, the second most important market after China, sales were 14 percent below the same month last year.
In Germany's home market, deliveries slumped by almost a sixth.
Brand sales chief Jürgen Stackmann attributed the weaker figures from July mainly to two fewer sales days in Europe.
In addition, there has been a reluctance to buy in some core markets due to "changed economic conditions".
In addition to the situation around the coup attempt in Turkey, the British vote to withdraw from the EU has also weighed, explained a spokeswoman.
In the U.S., where the exhaust fair first escalated, there is also a minus.
Great support for VW is the Chinese market.
Overall, the mainly affected house mark of the Lower Saxony continues to come through the diesel scandal anyway: After the first seven months, there is only a delivery reduction of 0.9 percent to 3.37 million vehicles.
Group sales chief Fred Kappler was able to continue to show an increase in sales from January to July.
Volkswagen delivered 1.3 percent more vehicles during this time, and 5.904 million worldwide.
Volkswagen had manipulated exhaust gas tests with software.
In Germany alone, VW is calling around 2.5 million cars for retrofit into the workshops.
In the course of the conversion, VW reaffirmed that the conversion of the cars has no negative effects.
After the conversion of the vehicles, there is no deterioration in fuel consumption, CO2 emissions or engine performance.
The car manufacturer issued a "guaranteed" certificate to the customers after the conversion, but does not speak of a "guarantee", as it was said on Friday in company circles.
The reason is legal subtleties.
"Focus" had reported that Volkswagen was now granting its German customers a guarantee for converted diesel models.
VW representatives in the Federal Ministry of Transport confirmed this last Monday.
It has been confirmed by the Department of Transportation that there has been this meeting.
"VW must fully comply with the requirements of my commission of inquiry and the KBA."
"Deviations from this will not be accepted," German Transport Minister Alexander Dobrindt (CSU) said, according to the ministry.
VW has assumed the guarantee that fuel consumption, CO2 emissions or engine performance do not occur after the conversion and all type-approval-relevant vehicle values remain unchanged.
Volkswagen itself does not use the term "guarantee", as it was called in corporate circles.
With the broad term "guarantee", Volkswagen sees the possibility that, for example, half a year after the conversion, customers come to a workshop with an engine failure - and could attribute this to the new software, even if the damage has nothing to do with it.
Even in the customer's interest, it is important to avoid the fact that there is an uncertainty with regard to possible warranty regulations in the case of any other damage, which has nothing to do with the emissions scandal, as it was said.
That's why VW doesn't use the term "guarantee."
