The president of the Federal Association of German People's Banks and Raiffeisenbanken (BVR), Uwe Fröhlich, had declared at the end of June: "The threshold to get negative towards the private customer is very, very high".
The competition is fierce, and it threatens the churn of investors to the competition.
Customers in Germany will not accept this in breadth.
Destination records: who actually buys records?
Lonely middle-aged men love plates.
Before rushing to the comment part to litter it under the pseudonym Non-AlleMenMen of age with harsh insults, this statement derives from up-to-date data.
According to YouGov, the much-discussed resurgency of records is not due to a boom in millennials who want to welcome the news of physical items, but due to midlife nostalgia.
Those who have recently purchased a slab are, in all likelihood, between the ages of 45 and 54.
In fact, the 18-24 age group is the most unlikely buyer group.
It’s also not a horde by hobby collectors - it has an emotional significance: older record buyers are more likely to keep their feelings for themselves (56% vs. 53%) and enjoy spending time alone (69% vs. 66%).
But are YouGov's results correct?
If this blog were a news report on television, the camera would follow me as I walk in the middle of a bustling street in Soho, wearing a modest grey suit, and stubbing wildly before stopping, holding my hands and saying something determinant like, "So, let's take a closer look."
Well, let's take a look at it.
Even if the heart of Sohos is slowly losing its charm - hotels, restaurants and offices are now filling the buildings where once independent vendors were based - there are still a handful of fantastic slab specialty shops.
I'd presumably ask the cameraman to turn off the camera for the next part where I'm standing in close proximity to different men in such shops and try to assess their age and mind through observation.
Stuart from Glasgow, 55, whom I follow from Sister Ray (data count: two middle-aged women, three middle-aged men) to Reckless Records (data count: eight middle-aged men, one woman, presumably around 20), is one day because of a meeting in London and goes through the shops to fill the few hours.
He's a collector, I ask?
"I'm guessing already," he says.
I've got about 3,000 or 4,000 records.
These records, pictured above (he sent me the snapshot by e-mail, I didn't follow him home), line the walls of his living room.
The reason he owns such a considerable number of albums and singles is the result of his disposable income: he is now able to buy records he couldn’t afford when they showed up and again to buy items he sold when he was young and broke.
"Many things I'm buying are from the late '60s, early '70s that appeared when I was 11 or 12. Things I presumably was a little too young to buy from me," he says.
He agrees that those who buy records are rather introverted and like to spend time on their own, adding: "I don't smoke and I don't drink very much.
That's my truck.
Wez, 25, who works at Sister Ray, also believes that many of the customers he meets apply to the YouGov profile.
However, he has also noticed a new wave of people influenced by the media hype, people who have heard about the comeback and are prompted to buy their old records again.
Of the conversations I've had, people usually got rid of their collection around 1998 or 1999.
Customers who once sold their records to buy CDs are now selling their CDs to buy their records again," he says.
That would explain the boom on supporters of the plate revival of a certain age.
But what about the supposed emotional significance of records?
It only takes a few seconds in a store like Phonica to realize that record stores provide a soothing community of like-minded people.
According to Wez, some of the older customers took offense to the contactless payment system.
Some record fans feel overwhelmed by the digital world and want to retreat to the stability of familiar record sleeves, from objects that evoke happy youth memories.
"I think at times it can be the filling of an emptiness or the possession of material possessions as some kind of comfort," Wez says.
That's the case with me as a collector and I think it's the case with a lot of people.
It's an easy way to engage mentally.
"We're like their social workers!" an employee flutters from a slab stack.
An ICM poll in April revealed that nearly 50% of people who bought a record last month haven't listened to it yet.
The survey also found that 41% own a turntable that they never use, while 7% of those who buy a record don't own a turntable.
At this point in the news report, I'd presumably look very worried as I scroll through a stack of Bossa Nova compilations.
Jonny, 42, who works at Sounds of the Universe (data count: three middle-aged men), has noticed a "trophy aspect" of the revival.
"Someone came in here only recently and said, "I don't own a turntable, but I'd like to buy a Radiohead record so I can put it on the shelf,"" he says.
This isn't a big percentage, but it's definitely happening.
More product-heavy, less music-heavy.
We've got people who say, "I don't have a turntable."
These are younger people, not older ones, who are slowly coming in.
Fopp, who has devoted almost an entire floor to the records, has a more colorful audience when I walk in.
There are couples around 70, tourists taking photos, teenage boys and middle-aged women.
Even if the atmosphere is a little less relaxed than in the aforementioned stores - Wild Beast's latest synthesizer and sex-packed pop instead of experimental jazz roars out of the loudspeakers - it's good to see such diversity among customers.
None of them look particularly lonely, but gnawing psychic traumas are difficult to detect when standing next to someone for 30 seconds.
Elanora, 27, has been walking around the store for several minutes, looking at the variety of records for sale.
She's doing a window-shopping rather than a large-scale shop.
She doesn't earn much, so collecting records isn't a real option, she says.
It'll cost a lot.
It's easy to listen to music from a computer, or in any other way, but the beauty of records is..." it drifts off with a sigh in love.
I don't know how to explain it - it's really unique.
My last stop is a store that is said to have inspired a new generation of record lovers: Urban Outfitters.
As I appear to pattern the jeans hotpants, I'm observing a group of young teenage girls grouped around Polaroid cameras for a moment before a father and his daughter consider buying a Crosley turntable.
On the left is a stairwell, a wall full of records by Adele, Jeff Buckley, Amy Winehouse and Fleetwood Mac: a mixture of contemporary music and classics, the essential records for an average collection.
No one looks at them - perhaps because they're just purely decorative, some so far up that it's impossible to touch them.
It's pure "plate art."
But considering that any attraction for these items could mean more money for the industry and send some customers to the depths of Soho for more, there's nothing to complain.
It's no surprise that a demographic that has more time and money than others is also the one spending the most on luxury items such as plates.
Even though my research revealed that YouGov’s results were correct, they disregarded, at least in record stores in Soho, the large number of female collectors and record lovers who exist in reality, record stores, and in online forums.
These people presumably work hard at school or offices.
They're certainly not spending their Thursday tomorrow strolling through Berwick Street's record stores and flipping through slabs, while avoiding the staring gaze of an eerie, 30-year-old fake presenter towering over the shoulders of middle-aged men.
The Greenland shark is the longest-living vertebrate - video report
Scientist says that the Greenland shark has the longest lifespan of all vertebrates on the planet.
