French court rejects break over demolition of shops in jungle refugee campaign
A French court has rejected an offer by authorities in Calais to tear down dozens of makeshift shops and restaurants in the Jungle of Calais where thousands of refugees live.
At a court hearing this week, authorities in Calais argued that the state should be empowered to tear down 72 provisional shops where people within the warehouse sell goods, from cigarettes to energy drinks, offering hairdressing services or selling tea for 50 cents (40 pence) or simple meals for a few euros in makeshift restaurants.
Calais Prefecture argued that these places should be demolished because they would represent a parallel economy from which no taxes would be paided and which pose a fire and medical risk.
Humanitarian groups told the court that the shops and restaurants were important, as the free meals offered by the state-backed association and other groups would not provide enough food for the growing numbers in the warehouse.
In addition, they stated that the makeshift shops and restaurants would often provide accommodation and free meals for people in need.
A court in Lille ruled that there was no legal basis for expulsion of people running the 72 provisional dealings, but acknowledged that Calais prefecture's concerns were "absolutely comprehensible."
Judge Jean-François Molla said the grocery stores, cafes and restaurants would play a much bigger role than merely feeding people who "live in extremely precarious conditions."
He said that the areas provide quiet meeting places between refugees and volunteers.
One of the places threatened by tearing down was the Jungle Book Kids' café of the camp, which operates non-non-professional and offers 200 meals a day, English and French classes, as well as advice on asylomy for the hundreds of vulnerable, unaccompanied minors in the camp.
More than 170,000 people have signed the petition to uphold Kids' Café.
In a letter to the Guardian, it was said that the closure "would be a disaster for these children, of whom the youngest are eight years old."
The migrant's two aid agencies Held Refugees and L'Auberge estimated on Friday that the number of people living in the jungle camp had risen to over 9,000 people - the highest number so far - after a steep increase in new arrivals over the course of the summer.
