Group joins start-up for live video game broadcasts
Watching other people playing video games is a huge trend - Microsoft also wants to take advantage of this.
The technology group is buying start-up Beam, which specializes in this hobby.
How much money's money's going on for the takeover, Microsoft didn't disclose on Thursday.
Beam only started with his offer in January and already has more than 100,000 users.
You can watch others play, but also intervene themselves.
For example, they can give up missions to players and juxtapose enemies as well as provide equipment.
The ply giant Activision Blizzard is launching a new HD video service for the booming world of e-sport.
On the road to the global media event, Facebook is supposed to help.
The advertising industry is already listening.
The employees of the start-up, which so far is based in Seattle in the US state of Washington, are moving to the Microsoft headquarters in Redmond in California, according to Beam co-founder and CEO Matt Salsamendi.
For the customers, therefore, nothing should change through the takeover.
Beam will be integrated into the video game department around the Xbox console, according to Microsoft.
