Here's the Enforcement Trailer
Germany's razors have to adapt to the next generation of speedometers: In various federal states, including in Hesse and Brandenburg, new lightning devices are being tested, which are intended to convict tempo sinners particularly effectively.
The futuristic-looking Enforcement trailers of the company Vitronic from Wiesbaden can, for example, be used at accident centers, where it would be too dangerous for mobile measurement by the police.
For example, at motorway construction sites or bridges to be refurbished with a temporary speed limit, there is often no necessary infrastructure to ensure compliance with the speed limit with a stationary speed camera.
However, a permanent mobile structure can be too dangerous or complicated.
The Enforcement Trailer is self-sufficient: its battery lasts five days with uninterrupted measurement operation, the case data is transmitted encrypted via a modem.
With laser measuring technology, all vehicles can also be recorded simultaneously across multiple tracks.
Variable speed limits as well as drive-through bans according to time, lanes and vehicle class can also be monitored.
According to the manufacturer, you can move the trailer with any car with a towbar in a raised state.
So that it is not simply stolen, it is lowered during measurement operation, so that the wheels disappear into the armored and, incidentally, also bullet-proof outer shell.
In addition, a built-in alarm system is intended to protect the speed camera from vandalism.
According to Vitronic, the trailer has been tested in various federal states since 2015 and is now used.
Foreign authorities are also flashing with the new technology: France has recently reordered 100 pieces, according to an order of 150 pieces last year.
Britain picks up silver in 7-time rugby after Fiji Masterclust takes gold
Britain's impressive performance at rugby reached an abrupt halt at the final hurdle in the Fijis in Thursday night's final when they were beaten with a result of 43-7 at Deodoro Stadium here in Rio.
Fiji awarded a master class in handling, side-stepping, follow-up, line running and raw power to win gold - their first Olympic medal of any color.
Defeat was sweetened by the not-too-bad consolation prize of the silver medal - Britain's fourth that day - and they could be quite proud of their efforts in this tournament, in which they beat New Zealand, Argentina and South Africa.
Boks' clear defeat, 7-5 in the semi-finals on the same day, could fall back on them in the final.
But regardless, Fiji would have beaten a 15-man team at his best in that form.
It could be consolation for GB - but probably won't - that at least one Briton will have a gold medal hanging around his neck tomorrow: Fiji's head coach Ben Ryan, the former boss for England from Wimbledon.
GB had a difficult path behind it.
Eight years ago they lost every game at the Adelaide Sevens under Ryan.
Here they lost only one.
This slouch will hurt, but the progress is amazing.
Fiji started wonderfully and blew off GB's attacks out of fun.
It felt for captain Tom Mitchell and Co. like running into moving buses. The World Series winners went up 5-0 when Osea Kolinisau foughted over in the corner.
With another four minutes of play, Jerry Tuwai added yet another attack from the right-hand corner, which he converted.
12-0 and GB barely touched the ball.
