Nakarawa added a new one since kick-off and there was still over a minute of playing time ... in the first half.
Vatemo Ravouvou squeezed through further weak tackles from the GB team and stormed up to the line to score.
The conversion yielded a 29-0 until the pause.
It's like Fiji was born to play the 7, while GB is still learning.
That's the way it is.
GB made fundamental changes at half-time and closed the fijian gap...for a full four minutes, which was good for the circumstances of the first half until Josua Tuisova went through after a brilliant interim play by Fiji.
Dan Norton started an attempt for GB, making a touchdown in the left corner for a 36-7, but the game has been going on for a long time in favour of the Southern islanders, whose fans sanged, waving flags and dancing in the stands.
It wasn't hard to admire Fiji's playful approach.
Mata Viliame put the icing on the cake with another attempt in the final seconds.
The whistle sounded, the fijis hugged each other and GB kicked in the turf scathing.
Earlier that day, Fiji had defeated Japan in the semi-finals with 20-5 and Britain pushed South Africa out of the race in a narrow semi-final match - Britain's conversion of the difference.
Kyle Brown put the Boks forward in the first half, but Dan Norton broke through the line with impressive footwork and scored a goal below the post in the second half.
A brilliant late tackling by Marcus Watson, Anthony's brother of the English 15-aside team, secured the win - and thus the silver medal.
Helen Glover and Heather Stanning inspired to rudder victory by battling the trainer's cancer
There won't be more emotinal gold than at these games and that'll mean something.
From the first rowing to the last, 220 hard hieves going down in history, Helen Glover and Heather Stanning found an additional meaning in their golden moment at Estacio da Lagoa.
Even if it was only the two who sat by the rudder, there were three voices in the boot. The echo of the coach Robin Williams, was all-present when they rowed to the second Olympic gold in the twosome without a helmswoman in succession, the first women in the history of British sport who set such a benchmark.
Her dominance was absolute, after all, her 39th undefeated effort, which is also unprecedented.
What we see are two brilliant female athletes gliding across the surface with the elegance of swans, which does not fully reflect the nature of triumph.
Behind every gold in rowing is a battalion of willing helpers who contribute to the incremental advances that make the difference and there was no one more helpful than the coach, who 30 months ago didn’t know if he would survive that day at all when he was diagnosed with cancer.
As he said, he's something risky.
I've had bladder cancer that's operable and they did.
If you don't, he'll spread everywhere.
It would've been a pretty dire situation.
It's changing the outlook a bit.
Williams was diagnosed in December 2013.
The trio were lucky that they could start treatment in early 2014, off-season, which kept the interruption to a minimum, if that's even the right term in such circumstances.
I needed money, so I had to get better soon.
It was an incentive.
I was in the hospital running around on the ward - 20 feet, 30 feet, 50 feet at a time when they said a few steps would suffice.
That's how it begins.
Rudding in itself is already a challenge, but that makes it an even bigger one.
In the immediacy of victory, her first thoughts went to Williams, who spent a nerve-wracking morning talking a little more than usual and trying to process his own fears.
Stanning, who, as captain at the Royal Artillery Regiment, was in a deployment to Afghanistan, said "I was an emotional wreck this week. That's maybe a bit extreme, but it means so much."
It only repeats how much that means to me, how much I and Helen and Robin have worked.
It's been three fantastic years with Helen and Robin.
We wouldn't be anyone without Robin, so there's a massive thank you to him. He's the best coach in the world.
I couldn't have wanted a better person to train or a better coach.
Helena Glover and Heather Stanning haven't lost a race in the last five years.
The pair had a tough rhythm that their opponents couldn't keep up with.
Even in the last 500 meters, when the teams from New Zealand and Denmark started catching up, Stanning and Glover had too much in reserve.
We were very trapped at the moment.
Helen shouted "stay in the trial, stay in the trial."
It's really important not to get carried away.
We've had a good start and at the same time we didn't want to do anything great.
We didn't want fireworks today, we just wanted to get a good result, get out and win the race, which we did as well.
The couple will take a break for a year before making decisions about their future.
Glover must appear at a wedding next week, her own with TV adventurer Steve Backshall.
Now she just wants to enjoy the moment.
I didn't want to say it was my last race.
That would've meant too much pressure.
In London, it took about six months to realize that I'm an Olympic champion.
It was all so new to me and I was so stressed.
It took about four minutes here.
It feels so much better," she said.
For 31 years, provided exemplary clarity
The managing director of the "Veitensteingruppe", Ruppert Göller, is retiring.
The association is considered "exemplary throughout Bavaria".
A small Franconian drinking water purpose association as a model for the large water producers.
Some time ago, it was said in the original tone in a contribution of the Bayerischer Rundfunk.
A little later, the water forum paid tribute to the government of Lower Franconia.
The work of the Veitenstein Group is "as exemplary throughout Bavaria".
Rupert Göller, who was an employee for 31 years in the Zweckverband, stands behind all of this, of which 28 years as managing director.
He has now retired at the age of 65.
The drinking water purpose association of the Veitenstein Group, based in Kottendorf, municipality of Breitbrunn, promotes about 150000 cubic meters of water annually and supplies 32 places and hamlets with about 5500 inhabitants.
Member municipalities are the municipalities Kirchlauter, Breitbrunn, Lauter, Ebelsbach and the city of Baunach.
There are water supply contracts for other cities and municipalities.
The current chairwoman, Breitbrunn's mayor Gertrud Bühl (FW), reminded that Rupert Göller was hired in 1985 as water attendant and appointed managing director of the Zweckverband in 1988.
"His tasks have been very diverse and they ranged from water pipeline construction and maintenance work to office and organizational activities, budget preparation, calculation of fees and contributions to emergency deployments on weekends," the chairwoman said.
She cited the commissioning and inauguration of the new wells (1994), land purchases in the area of the new wells (1998) and compensation of the farmers, the rehabilitation of the high tanks (1999), the new acquisition of a central computer steering (2004) and the partial renewals of water pipes.
There had been no standstill and there had always been innovations, because water meant life and drinking water was the most important food.
In her laudation she praised Göller's faithful, reliable and correct service over 31 years.
He has campaigned with a lot of passion for his water purpose association and he literally lived the water purpose association as if it were his own company.
A few weeks ago, as a real practitioner, he was able to present the success concept of the Veitenstein group for nitrate reduction in front of the water forum of the district of Lower Franconia.
He received a lot of recognition and praise for this.
Rupert Göller, for his part, praised water warden Alfons Griebel, who had worked with him for 28 years.
He's the best water warden and he's been a faithful squire.
Together we were an unbeatable team.
From his point of view, he described well construction and the restructuring of the Zweckverband as highlights.
However, the biggest challenge and the most difficult use were the burst of pipes at the high tank near Kirchlauter on 3 January.
Here it would have to work at minus 17 degrees all night so that the villages could be supplied with water.
Even the supply of Neubrunn could only be maintained once, because one filled the high container from Pettstadt with hoses.
The management of the drinking water association was handed over to Daniel Stürmer from Lauter, who succeeds Rupert Göller.
Soprano Anna Netrebko openly deals with her son Tiago's autism - and turns to other affected parents: "They must have the courage to talk about it."
"Medicine has made great progress," the 44-year-old told Italian newspaper Corriere della Sera.
When the disease had been recognized by her son, she believed she had to die.
However, eight-year-old Tiago has progressed well and is now learning privately in New York, where there are the best therapists.
"To give an example: when I ask him something that was two years ago when I sanged at Milan's Scala, he doesn't remember anything," Netrebko said.
He is the happiest person on earth.
Tiago comes from Netrebko's connection with the bass baritone Erwin Schrott.
She is now married tenor Yusif Eyvazov.
