Steel › Lichtblick for German Steel Industry
The crisis-ridden and energy-intensive German steel industry is spared from additional costs in the millions.
Financial burdens, which threatened the industry from green electricity funding, are off the table.
It remains with the exemption from the renewable energy levy at the existing plants for the so-called in-house electricity production in the German steel industry, German Economy Minister Sigmar Gabriel (SPD) told the "Westdeutsche Allgemeine Zeitung" (WAZ, Friday edition).
"I'm really happy that after two years of tough negotiations, we've finally been able to do that."
The European Commission had therefore initially planned to ask the steel producers, which use gases from the blast furnace to generate electricity for their own consumption.
The steel industry threatened additional costs of 120 to 240 million euros per year, according to the German industry association.
Gabriel told WAZ that there was now an agreement in principle with the EU Commission after talks with Competition Commissioner Margrethe Vestager.
Only when the generators in the steel mills are replaced, an EEG levy of 20 percent is incurred.
However, this is also not collected in the steel industry.
IG Metall welcomed Germany's unification with the EU Commission on self-generating electricity.
Thus, the status quo of the previous regulation is largely stipulated.
Gabriel's successful effort for the steel industry is of particular importance.
"In view of our 'Steel is future' campaign, this is a first step."
"Furthers must follow."
"Climate and employment targets must also be weighed when it comes to the reform of emissions trading," explained IG Metall CEO Jörg Hofmann in Frankfurt.
The steel industry in Europe has been suffering from overcapacity and considerable price pressures since the 2008 financial crisis.
Last year, the situation intensified further as the world's largest producing country, China, threw mass steel onto the world market in the face of weakening domestic demand.
European manufacturers accuse Chinese steel groups of price dumping.
Over the course of this spring, the situation has eased somewhat.
On the one hand, imposed import tariffs have an effect on import steel, on the other hand, the economic outlook in China has brightened.
It is unclear how sustainable the price recovery is.
Germany's industry primer Thyssenkrupp remained cautious on Thursday.
Recently, the prices would have stagnated again.
The industry is responding to the difficult situation with further cost reductions.
Thyssenkrupp did not want to rule out closures of individual plants.
At the end of 2015, around 86000 people were employed in the German steel industry.
In July, German steelmakers remain cautious despite rising prices and increasing orders.
With 3.4 million tons, they produced a good six percent less crude steel than in the same month of the previous year, as announced by the Wirtschaftsvereinigung Stahl on Friday.
After a brief increase in May, the quantity produced now declined for the second month in a row.
Since the beginning of the year, production has been two percent lower than in the previous year.
Thanks to an improved order situation, the association expects an increase in output in the coming months.
Community, mind, health: Singing does seniors good
Ursel Kipp has accompanied singing all her life: as a young girl she was in the school choir, in her teaching studies she took the subject of music, and today the 78-year-old sings in a senior choir.
"Singing moves me - emotionally and physically," she describes.
After a rehearsal, she feels balanced, cheerful, and more alive overall, she says.
By the way, she is doing something good for her health and spirit.
Singing is a great hobby for seniors from several perspectives, says Erhard Hackler from the German Senior League.
Most seniors sing in the chorus and share with like-minded people the joy of the music, Hackler describes.
By being together regularly, senior citizens can establish and maintain social contacts.
Because often social and familial structures dissolve in old age, a chorus is then an ideal opportunity to spend time with other people.
Ursel Kipp has also had this experience in her choir: "To discuss and organize, to be able to rely on the others and to learn from each other - a choir is a very special form of togetherness".
Singing can be exhausting in a positive sense, says Hackler.
In doing so, senior citizens train a deep and controlled breathing.
Those who demand their lungs on a regular basis do not get out of breath so quickly even in everyday life.
Even when it comes to maintaining the sound of the voice in old age, singing is an optimal hobby.
Singing often has a very special effect on older people, says Ursula Lenz of the Federal Working Group of Senior Citizen Organizations.
In the older generations, singing together had a much higher priority, it was part of everyday life, both at school and at leisure, she explains.
Lenz often observes that singing or listening to song in older people evokes many memories, often associated with positive feelings of her youth.
Through song lyrics and especially melodies, even people with dementia remember long past events.
When singing, the head is also demanded a special feat, explains Lenz.
At the same time, focusing on one’s own voice and on a predetermined rhythm while adapting to the other singers – this is a great cognitive challenge.
Especially older people, who live alone, practice focusing not only on themselves.
Another positive aspect is the recognition you experience by singing in the choir, says Hackler.
An avid audience is great praise.
Self-esteem also grows through singing in the choir.
To showing yourself: If I practice for something for a long time, I get better and grow beyond myself - strengthening this experience.
Good reasons to get started singing, so there's enough.
If you want to try a new hobby, you shouldn't hesitate - even if you think you have little talent, advises Lenz.
You don't sing alone - with 50 singing people, who hears when there's a ton next to it?
In search of the right offer, seniors have different options, says Hackler.
If someone from the circle of acquaintances sings in a senior choir, you can just connect.
Many cities also offer overviews of the choirs in the area.
If you want to sing in a church choir, you can contact the congregation or the church leader directly.
Many senior choirs can also be found over the internet.
If you do not have access to the Internet, you can ask the family, friends or neighbors for help with internet research.
If you want to sing, you should definitely join a chorus, advises Hackler.
Singing is good for the soul and has something liberating - that's true for all ages.
Cats react more sensitively than hangovers to cries of the juven
Biologists in Hanover have investigated the effect of calling cat babies for adult animals.
To her surprise, the meowing did not leave the males cold - however, in contrast to the females, they always behaved the same.
Female cats can recognize the degree of arousation of cat babies by the meowing of the little ones.
They react ten percent faster to sounds of kittens, who imagine themselves in great distress, as scientists from the University of Veterinary Medicine and the Hannover Medical School (MHH) have found out.
Cats also react to the calls of kittens - but always the same.
"Apparently, the acoustic differences between the meowing of less and highly aroused juveniles are either imperceptible or insignificant for the males," said biologist Wiebke Konerding of the MHH.
She is the first author of the study, which was published this Friday in the journal "BMC Evolutionary Biology".
In a previous study, the research team had already found that the duration and pitch of kitten screams differs with the urgency of their need for help.
A total of nine adult hangovers and eight kittens from animal husbandry of the MHH were observed.
On the one hand, they were auditioned with shouts from little ones, who were taken out of the throwing box for three minutes and separated from mother and siblings.
On the other hand, they were confronted with screams of more aroused kittens, which were additionally lifted.
The researchers consider their results to be meaningful despite the small sample.
"All females, even those who didn't yet have their own offspring, reacted differentiatedly to the screams," Konerding said.
They turned more quickly to the loudspeaker when this called of the much more aroused kittens was playing.
This ability may be congenital or develop with sexual maturity.
"One surprise was that the males reacted at all," the biologist said.
In the past, scientists have already examined the reactions of women and men to baby noises in humans on several occasions.
Some studies also showed that women were more sensitive to infants, regardless of whether they were mothers or not, reported biologist Marina Scheumann.
Podolski reaches for Supercup with 'Gala'
Hakan Sükür is a football legend in Turkey.
An arrest warrant has been issued in Turkey against former football star Hakan Sükür.
He is accused of being a member of a terrorist group.
For the first time after the coup attempt, an important football match will take place in Turkey again.
Before the duel of arch-rivals between Besiktas Istanbul and Lukas Podolskis Galatasaray Istanbul for the Supercup in Konya, the official line is unequivocal: "Business as usual" is announced.
Even at the imposition of the state of emergency after the overthrow attempt in July, the government asserted that the everyday life of ordinary citizens should not be affected.
However, this does not apply to those who are suspected of belonging to the movement of the preacher Fethullah Gülen, who is blamed for the coup attempt.
There is no stopping before football either.
Prosecutors issued an arrest warrant against former Starkicker Hakan Sükür.
According to this, Sükür, who now lives in America, is accused of being a member of a terrorist group.
Sükür is said to be supporters of preacher Fethullah Gülen, who is blamed for the coup attempt by President Recep Tayyip Erdogan.
Although the state of emergency, which was initially imposed for 90 days, still applies until October, in fact this has almost no effect on the life of most Turks.
Nocturnal "democracy guards" in public squares across the country Erdogan declared an end on Wednesday.
The sport - like the whole country - is divided into supporters and opponents of Erdogan.
Especially fans of the working-class club Besiktas do not traditionally count among the president's friends.
What unites the majority of Turks, however, in view of dramatic experiences in history, is the strict rejection of military coups.
Even the fans agree - regardless of whether their heart beats for Galatasaray or Besiktas.
With cup winner "Gala" it will be particularly important again to Lukas Podolski.
In the past season, the Cologne player proved his sporting value: With 17 goals and nine templates in all competitions, the 31-year-old ended his first season as his club's top scorer.
In the final against Fenerbahce, he scored the 1:0 victory goal.
Fanatik newspaper hailed him: "He was the best on the pitch."
He completed the season in an excellent manner and secured the trophy for his squad.
Thanks to "Poldi," the disappointing season for the record champion found a conciliatory conclusion.
After the preliminary round knockout in the Champions League, the "Lions" also retired early in the Europa League.
In the Süper Lig, it was only enough for sixth place.
The season wasn't good.
The cup was the only way to win anything.
"That's what we've done and that's what's the most important thing," Podolski said.
For Master Besiktas, the game year went better.
Mainly thanks to Mario Gomez, who laid the foundation for the 14th title with 26 goals of the season as Süper-Lig goalscoring king.
That's also why fans continue to hope for the 31-year-old's return.
After the coup attempt, Gomez, who was under contract with AC Florence and loaned to Besiktas, had announced that he would not return after his European Championship holiday because of the political situation in the country.
"If Mario wants to come to us, he just has to say it, "Kicker" club boss Fikret Orman said.
However, the striker would have to be prepared to forego around a third of his annual salary estimated at 7.5 million euros, Orman clarified: "We can't pay his salary at Florence".
Despite doping: These athletes still have medal chances at Olympics in Rio
34-year-old Justin Gatlin has already been banned twice for doping (2001 and 2006).
Rio de Janeiro - Whistleblower Julia Stepanowa is not allowed to start in Rio.
Other convicted athletics sinners can only laugh about this - they are competing for medals in Rio.
Doping continues to determine the headlines at the Rio Olympics.
Russian swimmer Yuliya Yefimova, who had sued her starting right shortly before the Games after a doping suspension, was mercilessly whistled and booed at her start (100 meters chest).
There was a quirky scenery: With an attached smile, she was still strutted into the hall and had provoked with her appearance.
When it was determined that she won't get gold, the audience broke out into a jubilant storm.
But there are numerous other athletes who compete at the Rio Olympics despite the doping past.
Here's an overview:
Justin Gatlin (100, 200, 4x100-m-season/USA): The 34-year-old is one of the most controversial figures in athletics.
He has already been banned twice because of doping (2001 and 2006 to 2010).
Since his spell, the Olympic champion of 2004 and ex-world champion runs even faster than before, he is the world's number one in 9.80 seconds over 100 m and is regarded as Usain Bolt's biggest challenger.
Shelly-Ann Fraser-Pryce (100, 4x100-m relay/Jamaica): The two-time Olympic champion and seven-time world champion had to serve a six-month lockdown in 2010 after she has been proven taking the painkiller Oxycodone.
She had severe toothaches after surgery, the 29-year-old defended.
Sandra Perkovic (Discus/Croatia): In 2011, the Olympic champion of 2012, world champion of 2013 and European champion from 2010 to 2016 tested positive for the banned stimulant methylhexanamine.
Perkovic denied the knozen capture and was banned for six months.
The fourthest litters this year go to the account of the big gold favorite.
LaShawn Merritt (200, 400, 4x400-m-season/U.S.): The Beijing Olympic champion and two-time world champion over 400 m, had tested positive in 2010 and been banned for 21 months.
A means of penis enlargement was responsible, Merritt asserted.
This year, the 30-year-old is the fastest man in the world so far over 200 and 400 m.
Iwan Tichon (Hammer throw, Belarus): Hardly an athlete's medals were subsequently revoked.
Tichon lost his 2005 World Cup title nine years later, after testosterone doping was detected.
The 40-year-old also had to give up Olympic silver in 2004 due to steroid doping and the 2006 European Championship title due to testosterone abuse.
He received the bronze medal of the 2008 Olympic Games, which had also been revoked, due to a ruling by the International Court of Sport CAS.
With 80.04 m, he ranks second in the world rankings.
Liu Hong (20 km walking, China): The world record holder and gold favorite only tested positive in May, but is still allowed to start in Rio.
The 29-year-old world champion found evidence of taking a banned dietary supplement, the blocking of the world anti-doping agency WADA was mild and expired on July 13.
Bombing terror in Thailand - Austrian woman injured - Several dead
The Austrian embassy in Thailand is in contact with the woman, Foreign Ministry spokesman Thomas Schnöll said Friday morning.
She suffered lacerations and was treated in the hospital, but had already been able to leave it again.
She planned her journey home at the beginning of September.
It is still unclear for the time being whether Austrian citizens are also affected by the further explosions on the holiday island of Phuket as well as in the southern cities of Surat Thani, Phang Nga, Trang and Chumphon.
The Ministry of Foreign Affairs is in constant contact with the local authorities in this regard, according to Schnöll.
Two bombs had first exploded in a night-of-off district in Hua Hin, about 200 kilometers south of the capital Bangkok, on Thursday evening.
According to police, one Thai woman was killed and about 20 people were injured, including the Austrian, three Germans, three Dutch and three Italians.
On Friday morning, two explosive devices went up again, according to the authorities, another person was killed.
A bomb blast was also reported from Phuket in the morning.
During the detonation close to Patong bathing beach popular with holidaymakers, a taxi driver was injured, according to polices.
In Surat Thani on the country's south-east coast, another bomb exploded, which killed a municipal employee.
According to the authorities, the explosive device was blown up in a flower bed in front of the precinct of coastal police.
Finally, detonations in Phang Nga and Trang on the south-west coast, as well as Chumphon on the south-east coast, claimed several injuries.
It is believed to be related to the explosions in Hua Hin, it said.
"The bombs carry the same handwriting," army general Danai Kritmethavee told reporters Friday morning.
“We believe it’s a coordinated attack.”
It is too early to comment on possible motives, but it is probably the actions of Muslim rebels.
Thailian mediaries also wanted to recognize in the attacks a pattern that is typical for the bombies of Muslim separatists in the south of the country.
Thai police, on the other hand, stated that the attacks were not related to the Islamist uprising movement or to international terrorism.
"This is only local sabotage limited to limited areas and provincies," said a spokesman in Bangkok.
Rather, it is assumed that the bombs were laid by opponents of the ruling military junta.
In the past few days, the authorities have received intelligence evidence of imminent attacks in the south of the country - but they have not been informed about the exact time and places.
It is still unclear which group was behind the attacks.
Police instructed their officers across the country to strengthen security around major government buildings as well as airports, train stations, tourist attractions, restaurants and amusement neighborhoods.
There are always minor bombings in Thailand, but they aren’t usually directed against tourists.
In Hua Hin lies a palace used for years by Thailand's King Bhumibol Adulyadej.
After the current bomb explosions, the Austrian Foreign Ministry recommends on its homepage to strictly comply with the instructions of the Thai security authorities.
High security risk (security level 3) applies to the provinces of Narathiwat, Yala, Pattani and Songhkla as well as Preah Vihear and the surrounding area.
After clashes between security forces and Muslim sections of the population, it is discouraged before non-essential trips to these provinces.
Increased security risk (Security level 2) applies to the rest of the country.
Attacks on Thursday and Friday took place a few days after a controversial referendum on a constitution drafted by the military junta.
This also gives the military great influence in the coming years.
Junta chief and Prime Minister Prayut Chan-o-cha said the bombs had been an attempt to wreak havoc as the country moves towards stability, an improvement in the economic situation and tourism.
The Military Unta, which seized power in 2014 after a period of bloody unrestories, is benefiting itself from having led the kingdom back to stability and order.
However, the military has also failed to get a grip on the smoldering uprising of Muslim rebels for years in the far south of the country.
More than 6500 people have already been killed in the conflict.
Almost a year ago, 20 people were killed in an attack on a popular Hindu shrine - most of them foreign tourists.
It was the bloodiest attack in the country's recent history.
The right-wing "identitarian movement" has been targeted by the Federal Office for the Protection of the Constitution.
According to several state offices, the constitutional protectors in the Confederation have also placed the group under observation.
"We see clues in the 'identitarian movement' for aspirations against the liberal democratic basic order," said Hans-Georg Maaßen, head of constitutional protection at the German Press Agency in Berlin.
In this way, immigrators are defamed by Islamic beliefs or from the Middle East in an extremist way.
That's why we're watching the movement.
The "Identitarian Movement" is a group with French roots, which has also been active in Germany since 2012.
She opposes "multi-culti craze," "uncontrolled mass immigration," and the "loss of one's own identity through over-alienation."
Constituent protectors in nine federal states - Bremen, Bavaria, Hesse, Berlin, Baden-Württemberg, NRW, Lower Saxony, Saxony and Thuringia - are already observing the "identitarians."
Maaßen said his authority had agreed closely with the state offices in the decision.
"Groups such as the 'identitarian movement' are trying to pick up their target group where it stands," the constitutional protection chief said.
In general, extremists try to address them with juvenile language, often with poppy music.
Islamists and right-wing extremists do the same.
That's not a good advertisement for one's own cause.
The propaganda is supposed to appeal to people emotionally.
Young people are particularly vulnerable.
That's dangerous.
Most recently, there had been reports from several federal states about contacts of the "identitarians" with AfD politicians.
Maaßen said he had no knowledge of this.
For the protection of the Constitution, possible contacts of this kind are only relevant at a certain point.
It depends on whether the respective party - in this case the AfD - by certain persons who are extremist, changes their target direction and thus becomes extremist.
The decisive factor is therefore whether such persons have a controlling influence on the party.
This is not recognizable.
In view of the significant increase in right-winning offenses and attacks on asylum seekers and refugee shelters, Maaßen said he would not yet speak of new right-wing terrorism in this context.
But we've noted in previous years that there may be right-wing terrorist structures.
There was the group 'Old School Society' or the Freitaler Group.
It may well be that further right-wing terrorist groups or micro-groups are forming, planning attacks against asylum seekers, against refugee shelters or attacks from other right-wing extremist motives.
However, Germany-wide or Europe-wide control of right-wing terrorism is not recognizable.
The previous groups were formed at the regional level or in social networks.
Maaßen expressed concern that many assaults went to the account of people who had not previously appeared in the right-wing scene.
That's the worrying thing.
Many of the suspects in attacks on asylum shelters were not previously known as criminals, politically motivated offenders or followers of the right-wing scene.
"Many people have committed crimes that previously had nothing to do with right-wing extremists, who may have been politically indifferent or, for example, elected the CDU, SPD or the left, but who then radicalized themselves through right-wing extremist sentiment."
Rio 2016: Robel Kiros Habte becomes internet hit
The Olympians or world records don't always determine the headlines at the Olympics.
That's according to Robel Kiros Habte's courageous appearance at the Summer Games in Rio de Janeiro.
At the Aquatics Center, the slightly overweight swimmer from Ethiopia swam so slowly that he was temporarily no longer featured in the TV pictures.
He simply disappeared - no matter how far the snippet was selected.
On the social networks, Habte subsequently became a viral internet hit.
In fact, the 24-year-old finishes his lead with a gap of 48 seconds and finished 59th out of 59 starters.
But it was rather his not exactly sporting figure who attracted the attention of the international media.
Why is the 24-year-old still taking part in the Olympics?
The Ethiopian received a special starting right from the international swimming federation.
This is being distributed in order to make swimming better known in underrepresented countries.
Since most athletes run in Ethiopia, Habte just wanted to do something different.
Preparation is everything: Robel Kiros Habte gets ready for the big race.
The first nicknames quickly made the rounds.
Some mediaries referred to him as the "robel of the whale."
Based on a "wealth belly", the English tabloid "The Sun" made fun of him with "Dad Bod".
His time, he struck only with a minute and almost five seconds over the 100 meters freestyle, didn't matter.
Comparisons with "Eric the Eel" were also drawn.
At the 2000 Sydney Olympics, Eric Moussambani's run attracted media attention.
Unforgettable was the appearance of the swimmer from Equatorial-Guinea, when he had to compete alone in a lead-up over 100 meters freestyle.
Frenetically, Moussambani was cheered on at the time.
The 22-year-old only studied swimming eight months before Olympia and had never completed such a long distance before the competition.
A seat located in the front row to the stunning architecture of the Central Library of Los Angeles
Learn about the history of the central library downtown, as the Society of Architectural Historians/Southern California Chapter holds a salon with Arnold Schwartzman and Stephen Gee, the authors of the new book "Los Angeles Central Library: A History of its Art and Architecture."
