Post enters the trade of electric vans
An electric delivery vehicle from Deutsche Post in action.
The interest of other companies in the vehicle is apparently great.
The Post plans to sell its electric vehicle StreetScooter as well.
Soon, the Group intends to start trading with the transporter.
From next year onwards, Swiss Post will also offer its electric vans on the free car market.
"We want to go on sale to third parties," a Post spokesman said Friday.
The company has been examining for months whether it is intended to sell its self-developed StreetScooter, with which letters and parcels are delivered, to other establishments.
Post board member Jürgen Gerdes had said that there were always questions, for example from craftsmen.
According to information from the news magazine "Der Spiegel", the Bonn-based company intends to start mass production of the vehicles shortly.
In the rose-throwing fabrication, the company is planning to build up to 10,000 cars per year per shift.
Gerdes had announced that the group wanted to significantly increase its fleet of electric cars and in the long terman targeted a complete conversion to environmentally friendly vehicles.
"We're going to take a very, very robust move towards 2020," he had said at the end of July.
I can imaginate that we're rearranging the entire fleet.
The StreetScooter has been active on Germany's streets since 2014.
GfK leadership steps down after disappointing business figures
The consumer research company GfK exchanges its boss Matthias Hartmann.
The resignations came suddenly - but not entirely surprising.
The difficult situation of the market research company GfK suggested that the chair of CEO Matthias Hartmann would become more insecure the longer the successes promised by him.
Turbulence at the head of the Nuremberg-based market research company GfK: After again disappointing business figures and the resignation of corporate management, the world's number five in the industry is facing a new start.
GfK CEO wants to leave the company at the end of the year.
Arno Mahlert, Chairman of the GfK Supervisory Councillor, will leave the Supervisory Board with effect from 12 September.
Gerhard Hausruckinger, a member of the Executive Board, will take over the leadership of the Executive Board as early as 1 September, the company said in a statement.
He is to officially assume the role of a "board spokesman" until a successor for Hartmann has been found.
As a reason for the resignations, the company cited "different views" on the long-term business policy orientation.
A company spokesperson declined to give details on Friday.
Who will lead the market research company in the future is currently still unclear.
In investor circles, it was said on Friday that the disappointment among the shareholders had recently been great about the remaining successes of the restructuring initiated years ago.
Mr. Hartmann has been at the GfK leadership since 2011.
"However, the fruits of the restructuring are not yet visible even after 5 years," it said.
In view of the recently poor GfK business figures, every shareholder will eventually become uneasy.
"If that were the case with BMW, that's what the Quandts would do, too," it said.
After setbacks in the previous quarters, the company had not been able to stoop its driving of loss even in the second quarter of 2016.
At 361.2 million euros, sales were 6.1 percent below the level of the comparable quarter of the previous year.
Compared to the first half of the year, sales shrank by 3.4 percent at 721.2 million euros.
Profit in the second quarter weighed above all on the company's lower value in view of its difficult economic situation.
The goodwill depreciation of 139 million euros, which became necessary in the Consumer Experience division, brought the bottom line a loss of 148.2 million euros.
In the second quarter of 2015, GfK had still generated a consolidated profit of 19.7 million.
Accidents - Bus slips in Krummhörn in ditch: Eight injured
Eight people were injured in an accident of a coach with 43 senior citizens as passengers on Thursday in Krummhörn (Aurich district).
Two of the inmates suffered serious injuries, police said.
The 66-year-old driver of the bus of a travel company from Eisenach had to dodge according to her, according to an oncoming truck, which drove partly on the oncoming carriageway.
The bus came to the right of the road and slipped into a ditch, as a spokeswoman for the Aurich/Wittmund police reported.
"Due to the location of the bus, the people could not leave the bus independently," she said.
The fire department freed the occupants.
Six ambulances and an ambulance arrived at the scene of the accident on Landesstraße 2.
The uninjured travelers were taken by a scheduled bus back to Krummhörner district of Pewsum.
There they should wait for a replacement bus of the travel company.
Investigators urged the truck driver and possible witnesses to report to police.
Fees per cubic meter of waste water for the Neudorf/Reuth/Welschenkahl sewage plant will be 3.44 euros instead of 2.10 euros so far from 1 October 2016; 3.36 euros (previously 2.26 euros) (previously 2.18 euros) for Zultenberg/Lindenberg/Lopp (previously 2.26 euros).
At the plant in Azendorf, the sewage fee will increase again at the latest when appropriate improvement measures become necessary.
According to an excessive calculation, the wastewater price in Azendorf will then be 4.37 euros per cubic meter.
As Mayor Bernd Steinhäuser (CSU) reported, during the approval of the budget by the Kulmbach district office it was determined that undercovers for these sewage plants.
Due to the legal basics, the Kasendorf market is obliged to operate the plants in a cost-covering manner.
From 1 October 2016, the citizens as well as the commercial and industrial companies from Welschenkahl, Neudorf, Reuth, Azendorf, Lopp, Lindenberg and Zultenberg will have to reach deeper into the wallet.
Fee increases range from 48 to 67 percent.
As administrative manager Herbert Schmidt pointed out to the panel, a recalculation of the fees for the sewage plants of the market municipality was necessary.
In the period from October 2016 to September 2020, overlaps or undercovers from the previous calculation period must also be compensated.
Schmidt justified the serious fee increases by saying that all three sewage plants were relatively small facilities with a very low sewage seizure, which ranged from 6800 to 12000 cubic meters per year.
Due to the low distribution basis, for example, expenses for necessary repairs of several thousand euros would have a serious impact.
In addition, throughwater volumes in all three plants are declining slightly.
Furthermore, in the previous calculation period for all three plants, renovation and repair expenses were incurred to a considerable extent, not least because of their age.
The plant in Azendorf is almost 30 years old, the plants for Welschenkahl, Neudorf and Reuth as well as Zultenberg, Lindenberg and Lopp for almost 20 years.
Schmidt pointed out that the mechanical ventilation system had to be replaced due to damage in the Azendorf wastewater plant.
In the Welschenkahl pumping station, the nearly 20-year-old conveyor systems had to be overhauled from scratch and after almost 20 years of operation, the first sewage pond had to be dredged, the sewage sludge was disposed of and an additional ventilation option had to be installed in order to be able to comply with the water law requirements in the future.
Schmidt: "For these extraordinary expenses came the updated care and maintenance costs, which must be taken into account accordingly in the new calculation period."
The same applies to electricity costs, which were still well below the current level with the previous calculation in 2011/2012.
Steinhäuser made it clear that in the case of the low sewage attack in the three plants, the expenses for operation and upkeep "fully penetrate".
According to Schmidt, the market town has been spared from large maintenance costs for the sewage plants over the past 20 years.
According to Steinhäuser, the situation for the above-mentioned plants will remain precarious in the long term, especially since the supply area of the Friesenbachtal waste water purpose association cannot be expanded by these plants, because they are deficient facilities.
Baulgia recorded inflation for the first time in 8 months
National statistics show that inflation in Bulgaria amounted to 1% in July on a monthly basis.
This is the highest (since June 2012) and the first above zero since October last year, when positive 0.2% were posted.
The annual average inflation of the last 12 months (August 2015 - July 2016) compared to the previous 12 months (August 2014 -July 2015) was -0.8% measured by the CPI.
Compared to June 2016, the consumer price index in July 2016 amounted to 101.0%, i.e. monthly inflation was 1.0%.
The inflation rate since the beginning of the year (July 2016 compared to December 2015) was -0.2% and annual inflation in July 2016 was -0.2%.
Compared to the previous month, a price increase of the following groups of consumer goods was observed in July 2016: food and non-alcoholic beverages (1.7 %); transport (1.8%); leisure and culture (6.3%); restaurants and hotels (1.2%); various goods and services (0.3%).
Prices fell for clothing and shoes (2.2%); furniture, home appliances and routine maintenance (0.5%); health (0.1%).
Prices remained unchanged for alcoholic beverages and tobacco, as in the previous month; housing, water, electricity, gas and other fuels; communication; as well as education.
Snapchat 'anime' effect criticised as Asian caricature
Snapchat says this filter is anime-inspired, but some observers say it's insensitive to races.
With its filter tool, Snapchat enables around 150 million daily users to change reality and play with identities in such a way that already borders on the absurd.
You can turn yourself into a pineapple, a dog, or figure that could be made of a Roy Lichtenstein painting.
The filters are blunt, tranquitorial tools that produce more than 30 million improved selfies per day.
All missteps go public.
Snapchat filters have attracted criticism in the past where they have been accused of the app promoting blackface or propagating white skin as an ideal of beauty.
When this week it suggested to some users a filter that gave them slit-eyes, false teeth and bloated cheeks, some critics called it a racist caricature of Asians - "yellow face."
They wondered whether these repeated controversies pointed to a bigger problem that the company has with diversity.
The news and indignance became widespread on Wednesday, with reports from The Verge and Motherboard, a day after Snapchat announced it would have disabled the feature.
The company offered an apology: the filter was intended as a tribute to anime characters rather thand not as a caricature of Asians.
But outsiders who have experienced racissus, the filter was reminiscent of hurtful clichés.
Others strongly opposed the anime comparison.
In an e-mail, Grace Sparapani, a Korean-American art student whose tweet about the photos were widely circulated, wrote that the filter "was uncomfortable in hurt and to say the least."
She added that "it is difficult to argue with the juxtaposition of a very disgusting Asian caricature and the filter effects.
It shows that the filter is not yellow-faced, but that the yellow face has been dragged into the derogatory extreme.
Snapchat isn’t the only company traversing these cultural stumbling wires.
American culture appears to be involved in an endless diversity and integration struggle, from conference rooms to Hollywood and the devices we all hold.
And Snapchat's large audiences of young people - who are more diverse in terms of race than their older counterparts - might be more likely to expect sensitivity.
If one of Snapchat's filters creates an image that's insulting to a user, "it's much more difficult that such stuff goes unnoticed than it used to," 25-year-old Katie Zhu said in an interview on Thursday.
On Thursday, Zhu, who works as a product manager and engineer for Medium, decided to delete her Snapchat account prompted others to do the same.
In an essay for medium and in a phone interview, she said she believes race-related controversy reflects a lack of diversity in hiring practices on Snapchat.
Zhu criticized the largely white, only male leadership and ended her essay with a hashtag: #DeleteSnapchat.
"Either she doesn't have various representations of people with other skin colors in her workforce so they can make such decisions," the Chinese-American Zhu said, "or they have people with other skin colors who work there, but aren't in positions where they feel safe or comfortable to say something about it."
Others share their opinions, a complaint that has mostly left Snapchat unanswered.
The company does not publish figures on the diversity of its workforce and points to its status as a private company.
On Thrusday, Snapchat declined to discuss the cultural backgrounds of its employees, but according to a spokesperson, the company recently hired a human resources manager focused on underrepresented demographics and internal integration efforts.
Zhu, for her part, said she's going to let her Snapchat account shut down, adding, "I'm wondering if they need more users like us to be able to say that's not O.K."
Military leadership falsifies ISIS intelligence information, congressional committee notes
United States Central Command military personnel changed intelligence to paint a more optimistic picture of the war against the so-called Islamic State in Iraq and Syria than would have allowed the actual conditions on the ground, an investigative committee of Congress reported in a report Thursday.
The preliminary report, prepared by a task force of the Republican members of the Armed Forces Committee, intelligence committees, and the defense budget commite, reports great dissatisfaction among Central Command intelligence analysts who reported how supervisors changed their assessments of American efforts in the fight against Islamic State.
Central Command, or Cencom for short, is the military headquarters in Tampa, Florida, which controls American military operations in the Middle East and Central Asia.
"Emergency information typically nodded off by senior Centcom commanders regularly painted a more positive picture of American efforts against terrorism than the actual conditions on the ground would have allowed and were consistently more positive than analyses of other intelligence services," a newspaper article cited the report as saying.
"What happened at Centcom is unacceptable - our soldiers suffer when bad analyses are presented to our political leaders," noted Ken Calvert, Republican House-Relative member.
The leadership flaws in Centcom extend to the top tip of the organization.
The ten-page report shows in-detached problems in 2014 and 2015 in the descriptions and analysis of American efforts in the training of the Iraqi armed forces.
Although there is no direct indication that government spikes under Obama have given instructions to change the reports, it shows how analysts faced the pressure from their supremacies to paint a more optimistic picture of the Islamic State's threat, also known as ISIS or ISIL.
"During the first half of 2015, many Central Command press releases, statements and statements before Congress were significantly more positive than the facts," the report noted.
For example, a Centcom relative publicly announced that a military reconquest of Mosul could begin as early as April or May 2015.
Mosul, Iraq's second-largest city, remains under Islamic State control.
"After months of investigations, so much is clear," Mike Pompeo, Republican congressman from Kansas, said in a statement.
From the middle of 2014 to the middle of 2015, the spikes of Central Command altered the intelligence reports to downplay the danger posed by ISIS in Iraq.
Republicans launched the task force after being leaked that analysts were worried about manipulation of reports on the Islamic State.
The report, published on Thursday, will still be completed by more detailed findings while the investigation continues.
In addition, further investigations of the Centcom reports are underway by the Inspector-General of the Ministry of Defence.
Democratic members of the Intelligence Committee on Thursday released their own findings, which were consistent with some of the conclusions of the Republican task force.
"In 2014 and 2015, Centcom created an overly restricted process for preparing intelligence assessments on ISIL and Iraqi security services," the highest-ranking Democrat at the committee, a member of Adam B. Schiff House of Representatives, said in a statement.
This procedure, according to Mr. Schiff, "delayed the publication of intelligence reports," damaging morale among analysts and "giving underagely little room for dissenting opinions."
But Mr Schiff and the Democrats said they had not found any evidence of White Hous intervention among the Centcom analysts regarding a "certainal political opinion."
Preliminary results of the congressional investigations were released Tuesday through "The Daily Beast."
Last August, the New York Times reported investigations brought by the Pentagon's Inspector-General, which have been commenced following complaints by Centcom analysts.
Lt. Cmdr. Patrick Evans, a Pentagon press secretary, said in a statement that the Department of Defense would not comment on congressional reports while the inspector general's investigation was still ongoing.
But he also stated that "experts sometimes have quite divergent opinions regarding the interpretation of compliant data, and that the intelligence services and the Ministry of Defense welcome a controversial discussion of these important issues of national defense."
Investigations have reversed effects beyond the issue of a successful aerial campaign under American leadership in Iraq and Syria (a suspection now made by a growing number of members of the Department of Defense).
Last year's accusations, however, raised the question to what extent President Obama can rely on Central Command for honest assessments of military operations in Iraq, Afghanistan, Libya and other crisis regions.
Criticism of secret information about the Islamic State is reminiscent of discouragements from more than ten years ago, when a cent-com analyst Gregory Hooker, wrote a document about how, contrary to the recommendations of many analysts, the then-governmen government under George W. Bush only provided for a small occupying force in Iraq and devoted little time to thinking about the aftermath of the 2003 invasion.
Mr Hooker also played a key role in the uprising against the current coverage of the Islamic State.
Last year, according to government agencies, the team led by Mr. Hooker found that, contrary to the government’s statements, air strikes against the refineries held by the Islamic State would not have a significant impact on the terrorist group’s financial situation, as they would have put temporary refineries into operation to sell oil on the black market.
However, this finding has not been published, the Times reported in September.
Centcom's then commander, Gen. Lloyd J. Austin III, has been criticized by members of the legislature because of his overly positive assessment of the war situation at a hearing before the Senate.
German Austin retired in April and was replaced by Gen. Joseph L. Votel at Central Command.
The U.S. wins fourth match in women's field hockey, a showdown with U.K. imminent
A day after scoring three goals during a 6-1 win over Japan, Katie Bam also scored the first two goals for the women's American field hockey team in a match on Thursday against India that the American women won by 3-0.
Five goals in two games was supposed to represent a memorable performance, but Bam (27) said she couldn't remember ever doing that.
"I don't know anything," she said.
I'm not one of those people who remembers their gates.
However, she knows this: The American women are at 4-0 in the Olympic competition and are on the same level in their group with the British women in first place, who remain unbeaten after their 2-0 victory against Japan.
Britain and the United States will meet on Saturday in the final game of the first round.
Both teams have already qualified for the quarter-finals, the game will decide which of the two within their group of six teams will gain first and second place respectively.
The first-place finisher will avoid the strong Dutch women a little longer, who lead the other group.
Whichever Saturday's game may turn out, the American women have sparked a surprising fuss through their success story, four years after they landed at the past London Games in the last of 12 places.
Bam was part of this team.
She grew up in Blue Bell, Pennsylvania, and for the first time at the age of three had a hockey stick in her hands (her two older sisters played the sport at Drexel University), at the age of 16 she became a U.S
She missed the Olympics in 2008 as she failed to qualify for the squad.
Then came the year 2012, in which everything went wrong for her and her team.
The following year Craig Parnham, an Englishman, became coach of the national team and with that, things began to turn.
"A change of culture, a mental change and a physical change," Bam said.
We didn't have the workload in London that we have now.
We've been able to take the rudder completely.
On Thursday night, Bam opened the Torreigen in the first half of the game with a back-and-heel blow from close-up.
India had gone into the match without victory in the tournament, but the team appeared surprisingly tough and held the score from 1-0 to the third quarter until Bam took the game into their hands.
She performed a sprint with the shallow ball over the left flank, then pulled into the middle and plucked the ball into the goal to give the Americans a 2-0 lead.
Melissa Gonzalez scored the final goal in the middle of the final quarter.
The shutout was the first of the whole tournament for U.S. goalkeeper Jackie Briggs, who has so far scored one goal in each of the previous matches.
She's having a run.
Just like Bam.
"We're in a good position," Parnham said, but how good will depend on the outcome of Saturday's next game.
In the midstitute of the rage of the Olympics, the Zen des trampolines
Wherever you are, however late it is, it's the Olympics.
The events in Rio de Janeiro feel consistent and inescapable, a frenzied relay between competition and pursuit.
It's like the Olympic torch itself is a burning baton passed from crossbower to swimmer to gymnast to triathlete.
You can't turn on the TV, read a newspaper, or listen while talking to your neighbor in the queue in the coffee shop without hearing about Simone Biles, Katie Ledecky or the dispute between Michael Phelps and Chad le Clos.
And then there's Logan Dooley.
Dooley, who is at the Olympics for the first time and is a swarm of women with dimples, could well have been under these well-known names broadcast to prime time, built up by sponsors and feel-good TV advertising if he were a swimmer, gymnast or even fencer.
But Dooley, 28, from Lake Forest, California, is competing for gold in Olympic trampoline gymnastics.
The Olympic flame doesn't illuminate every event the same.
Further from the center, partly in the shade are the sports, which are not universally accepted: air gun shooting, archery, badminton, taekwondo.
We'll always have breaststroke swimming and we're going to rule for a long time
But if I may modestly make arguments for my particular darling, then let me draw attention to the trampoline's discreet charm.
Although trampoline jumping has already been an Olympic event since 2000, when it made its debut in Sydney, it has been so sparsely perceived for the past 16 years that many who, I had questioned, didn't know that it's an Olympic sport at all.
That could also be regional ignorance.
Even though the U.S. sent athletes to compete, it has never won a medal in trampoline jumping and had an athlete who made it to the finals by 2012.
The Chinese and the Russians tend to share prices for the men and the Men, and the Canadians those for the women.
Dong Dong from China and Rosie MacLennan from Canada will be defending their gold medal from London this year.
Trampoline jumping has a certain outsider charm, partly because it sounds more suitable for the unenlightened ear for a clown school than that for the Olympics.
At this point, I should admit that I am not an expert, but just an earthbound enthusiast.
It has humoristic potential - the cartoonish trembling of his never-to-ending impact - but also the grace of gymnastics and diving, the two sports it essentially unites.
The word "trampoline" comes from the Spanish El trampoline, which means "springboard."
Emerging victors jump on a gigantic trampoline, shoot almost up to 30 feet into the air and have a series of falling movements (turning, piking and saltores) before landing on their feet.
According to the official Olympic program, they will be evaluated according to execution, level of difficulty and "flight time".
Thirty-two athletes from 17 countries, 16 men and 16 women, will compete against each other in Rio.
The most common Olympic vector is straight ahead.
Medal hopes have to go further in their pursuit of gold, faster, up and over, sometimes all the way to the destination, sometimes back and forth, up and down in the playground.
They not only chase the finish line or the exit wall, but also another sneering goal in the television broadcast: that of the world or the Olympic record.
The purposeful aspiration is noble, admirable, even fanatical - and it's enough to move even a spectator to tears.
But it's also what I've figured a little tiring even when watching these games.
The trampoline offers a respite from this.
The vector of the trampoline jumper is on top.
A forward movement is not the goal.
He or she bounces, seemingly weightless like an astronaut, goes up, then down, then effortlessly back up.
Again there is a competition, of course not without difficulty, but during the exercise there is no hectic attack on the target, no bulging into the distance, only the dreamy, elegant abolition of time and gravity.
It's as Zen as the competition at the Olympics can only be.
Women's qualifying and final rounds will take place on Friday afternoon, in streaming at 13.00 Eastern Time; those of the men on Saturday, also at 13.00.
NBC will broadcast the finals as part of an afternoon block on both days from 14.00.
In the midst of Olympic mania, they promise a quiet, nice moment, one that is likely to be overlooked in general tumult.
If the increase in consciousness can only be achieved through the attack and objectification of male athletes - as the male U.S. gymnasts recently suggested, according to The Wall Street Journal - then Mr. Dooley expects his new fan base wrestling.
'A generation protests' in Ethiopia, Long an ally of U.S
Violent protests in Ethiopia
Demonstrators calling for political changes in Ethiopia encountered violent opposition from the government.
Witnesses testify that dozens of protesters were killed during clashes with the police during exchanges of gunfire.
Is Ethiopia just about to collapse?
Over the past decade, it has been one of Africa's stabiest countries, a solid ally of the West with a fast-growing economy.
But in recent months, anti-government protests have shaken the country and expanded into more and more areas.
Last week, thousands of people stormed into the streets and demanded fundamental political changes.
The government's response was relentless, according to human rights organizations.
Witnesses stated that police officers shot and killed tens of unarmed protesters.
Videos in circulation about the protests presumably from last or early this year show security guards beating young people with sticks as they were forced to make a handstand against a wall.
The head of the U.N. Human Rights Agency is now calling for an in-depth investigation.
"It's always been difficult to keep this country together and in the future it's going to become even more difficult," Rashid Abdi, the project leader of the Horn of Africa of the International Crisis Group, told a research group.
Ethiopia is the most populous country in Africa after Nigeria and its stability is valued by the West.
American military and intelligence agencies are working closely with the Ethiopians to combat terror threats beyond the region, particularly in Somalia. Few countries in Africa, if at all, receive so much Western aid.
Ethiopia's economy expanded at a considerable pace.
Its infrastructure has drastically improved - there is a new commuter train in the capital Addis Ababa.
The streets are usually quiet, safe and clean.
Even if Ethiopia was anything but a role model for democracy - human rights organizations have continuously cited the oppression by the government - the opposition in the country has been curtailed, with dissidents effectively silenced.
Many were exiled or sent to prison, killed or driven into the vast expanses of the desert.
But that could change.
"If you oppress people and these have no other options other thand to protest, it breaks out," said Seyoum Teshome, a lecturer in Central Ethiopia.
The entire youth are protesting.
One generation is protesting.
There are numerous complaints that cover everything from land use to stranglement in terms of power of the ruling coalition.
After a widely criticized election last year, the ruling party and its allies were given the last seat of the opposition and are now controlling 100 per cent of parliament.
At the same time, tensions are rising along the border with Eritrea; a battle for this jagged, controversial line claimed hundreds of lives in June.
Analysts fear that separatists such as the Oromo Liberation Front or the National Liberation Front of the Ogaden, who have more or less been defeated in recent years, could exploit the turbulence and re-arm themselves.
Several factors explain why feelings of bitterness now explode after years of skewering under the surface.
The first seems harmless: smartphones.
Only in recent years many Ethiopians have been able to communicate by means of social media, as cheaper smartphones became more and more common and Internet services improved.
Even if the government blocks access to Facebook and Twitter, which it does at regular intervals, especially at protests, many people can still communicate through internet proxies that hide where they are staying.
Several young Ethiopians said they gathered for protests in this way.
The second factor is that there is more solidarity between the Oromos and Amharas, the two largest ethnic groups in Ethiopia.
Oromos and Amharas are not natural enemies.
For eons, the Amharans, who come from Ethiopia's predominantly Christian highlands, have prospered in politics and business while exploiting the Oromos, many of whom are Muslims and live in shallowland areas.
But even that's changing right now.
"We are in the process of coordinating under an umbrella," said Mulatu Gemechu, a leader of the Oromos.
The biggest protests took place in areas of the Amhara and Oromos.
Many Amharas and Oromos feel that Ethiopia is unfairly dominated by members of Tigray's ethnic group, who make up around 6 percent of the population and who are dominant in the military, intelligence services, commerce and politics.
The third reason for the unrest is the death of Meles Zenawi.
Mr Meles, a former rebel leader, was Ethiopia's prime ministor for 17 years until his death from an unpublished illness in 2012.
He was seen as a tactical genius, a man who could think around corners.
Analysts say he was particularly savvy at perceiving early signs of dissatisfaction and using special envoys to edit opponents and make them harmless.
"The current regime lacks this basic skill," said Mr Abdi, a conflict analyst.
Ethiopia's new prime minister, Hailemariam Desalegn, was selected for relatively opaque reasons to take Mr Meles' position.
Unlike Mr. Meles, who came from the Tigray region of Northern Ethiopia, Mr Hailemariam comes from the south.
Analysts say he doesn't enjoy the trust of the Tigray-controlled security services.
Many fear that the result will be more bloodshed.
The last time that Ethiopia experienced such turmoil was in 2005, after thousands protested against elections seemingly botched by the government and subsequently stolen, according to analysts.
During the ensuing raids, many protesters were killed, even if they were fewer than in recent months and this time of unrest passed relatively quickly.
Develop experts praised Ethiopia's leaders for their visionary infrastructure planning, such as the new commuter train, as well as their measurable advances in poverty reduction.
But obviously this has not prevented the intensification of internal resentment by the Ethiopian government.
And she's taking a dangerous ethnic form.
Last month, protesters in Gondar, an Amharian city, attacked businesses run by Tigray and hatred for the Tigray is becoming increasingly common on social media.
Analysts say the protests put the U.S. and other Western allies in a peculiar position.
The U.S. government used Ethiopia as a base for drone flights over neighbouring Somalia, even if it recently announced that it has closed that base.
While the West unequivocally wants to support democracy, it does not want its ally to collapse in an already volatile area.
"This," Mr Abdi said, "is a very thin tightrope."
Donand Trump declares his claim Obama founded ISIS was "sarcasm"
Donand J. Trump on Friday tried to dispel the last controversy surrounding his campaign by stating that his multiple-repeated claim that President Obama and Hillary Clinton were the "founders" of the Islamic State terrorist group had not been serious.
This clarification raised new questions about the communications capabilities of Republican presidential nominee whether he was able to communicate clearly and comprehensibly with the American public.
It also puts his comrades-in-arms trying to protect him during an eventful phase into the peculiar position of having to explain statements he couldn't have meant at all.
After making the hint at a campaign event on Wednesday night, Mr. Trump rowed back in relation to Thursday's allegation and insisted in interviews that he really didn't intend to indicate that the president and Mrs Clinton had created ISIS.
But in an early morning tweet on Friday, Mr Trump said he was simply sarcastic.
The tweet was the contrast to the previous day when Mr. Trump said in a series of interviews that he meant the allegation completely serious.
When Conservative radio presenter Hugh Hewitt wanted to help Mr. Trump the statement by reminding him that Mr. Obama wants to destroy ISIS, the Republican presidential nominee didn’t want to know about it.
The last storm of criticism pounces down on Mr. Trump as he has tried to steer the focus of his campaign because of falling poll numbers on the economic situation.
Mr. Trump and Mrs. Clinton gave speeches on the economic situation in Michigan this week.
On Friday, Mr. Trump tried to return to this issue again.
Reporting via Twitter, he warned that Mrs Clinton's proposals would lead to higher taxes and that he found her speech dull.
Mr. Trump has already put forward sarcasm in his defense.
At the end of July, he drew strong criticism when he said that the Russians were supposed to chop Mrs. Clinton's emails, but he later declared "Of course I meant sarcastically."
This method allows Mr. Trump to push the linguistic boundaries in politics and then row back as needed.
"I think it's one of his methods to test how certain messages are received by his fans, how they arrive, and then, if necessary, "clearing" depending on how the reaction turns out," said Ruth Sherman, a communications expert who observes the language of both candidates.
However, I also believe he is clever enough to keep his statements vague enough, so that they can be interpreted in different ways.
Instead of defusing the tone for the presidential election, Mr. Trump has taken an even corrosive tone as his poll numbers have declined further.
A new NBC/Wall Street Journal/Marist poll released Friday showed a leadership with a big lead in the states of North Carolina, Virginia, Colorado and a head-to-head race in Florida.
While Mr. Trump generally tries not to apologize for his provocative remarks, he frequently accuses his critics of distorting his statements or misunderstooding his humour.
But while he's increasingly the focus of attention in the final months of his presidential campaign, Mr. Trump's jokes have put his loyal comrades-in the uncomfortable situation of having to stand by him, regardless of how provocative his statements may be.
Rudolph W. Giuliani, former mayor of New York and one of Trump’s most prominent advocates, went to great lengths to defend the nominee today in a CNN interview on Thursday.
"I believe what he's saying are legitimate, political comments," Mr. Giuliani told him about the correctness of Mr. Trump's statements about whether Mr. Obama and Mrs. Clinton had founded the Islamic State.
It is true in this sense that ISIS was an almost unknown, small organization before Obama, he called it backbenchers, completely false and because it happened because he withdrew the troops from Iraq.
Most mainstream Republicans are trying to argue that the administration under Obama, in which Mrs Clinton served as secretary of state, allowed ISIS to spread by failing to aggressively control the situation in the Middle East.
Ahead of campaign events in Pennsylvania on Friday, Mr. Trump continued his attacks on the media and accused them of riding around on each of his words and being biased against him.
He mocked the "poor, miserable" television experts trying to understand him and said "You can't do it!".
Some of the Democrats scoffed at Mr. Trump's apology as just further evidence that he was unsuitable for the presidency.
Once about the price ousted from the market, Café comes back in Greenpoint
Verb Cafe was a shabby café in Williamsburg, where you could only pay in cash that didn't have Wi-Fi but served good coffee for 15 years.
Of course, prices have been ousted from its Bedford Avenue storefront.
Soap Cherie, which replaced Verb in the summer of 2014, sells soap in the form of cupcakes.
Locals mourned it as the last loss in a gentrifying city.
Artist Molly Crabapple wrote on Twitter, "I know New York is change, but I still can't believe that CBGB is dead, excluding the Chelsea hotel and verb is now a soap business."
But if New York is change, it's also reinvention.
A resurrected verb cafe opened in Greenpoint last November, less than a mile north of its original location.
The new verb has Wi-Fi and accepts credit cards, but still has some of the same employees and a similarly simple atmosphere.
The coffee is still good too.
The new owner of the verb is Cisco Rodriguez, a long-time collaborator of the old cafe, which is the owner and managing director of the new cafe.
He began working at Verb in 2001 and stayed there until the end.
When a customer offered him to provide funds for a new field office, he was excited to do it, he said.
Rodriguez, 36, retained a lot.
Verb used the same distributor for the bagels bought the same espresso machine model.
It even uses the same lamps from the old verb.
But Rodriguez said he also wanted to experiment.
He introduced new ingredients.
On the menu are sandwiches with bacon and cheese and there are garrinations such as capers and sautéed onions.
There are natural rice bowls.
The café no longer serves beer, there are fresh juices.
After nine months, Rodriguez said that he had noticed a steady stream of old regular customers.
"Many old customers have also been ousted by prices from Williamsburg," he said.
Recently on a Sunday Rebecca Oliveira was such a customer.
Oliveira, 34, used to live in Williamsburg and visited the verb regularly.
Even after she moved to Greenpoint, she would take her way.
After the verb closed, she gave up drinking coffee for a while, she said.
"I realized it was because of the coffee and the culture in the verb I liked," she said.
Now she drinks coffee again, even if on that day, instead of her usual iced coffee, she ordered a blended chai tea, which is another new addition.
Tom Rosenthal has been another long-standing and loyal customer since the verb began in 2003.
He went there before work and inevitably always ordered the same thing.
"I came quarter past seven, every day, like a Swiss pocket watch and they're preparing my everything bagel with butter," he said.
Photo Rodriguez said he wanted to experiment and add new ingredients to the menu.
These days Rosenthal still gets his usual order, but sometimes he replaces his everything-bagel with bacon, egg and cheese.
Rodriguez said that he didn't harbor a resentment that the soap business has taken up the old business space.
In fact, the owners of the Soap Cherie offered to produce a special soap for verb with the cold-brewed coffee of the cafe as an ingredient.
"I think we're still going into their offer," Rodriguez said.
And the traditional Polish residents Greenpoints have also accepted the cafe.
A new customer has translated the menu into Polish and Rodriguez said that he intends to exhibit it soon, so that his new neighbors feel welcome.
The location also has other advantages.
A local juice restaurant wants to fill and sell the cold-brewed coffee into the bottle.
There's a chanch that the cafe will expand onto a neighboring building.
For many customers, a new verb is enough to get their old favorites.
Joseph Witt was a regular customer and said that he was devastated when the café closed.
When the new cafe opened, Whitt said entering the venue and ordering a new coffee felt like a homecover.
"I had to smell this," he said.
Depressing eating of the economic crisis in "an ample meal"
In March 1933, shortly after his ascension to president, Franklin Delano Roosevelt took his lunch at the Oval Office.
As a foodie, President Roosevelt had a penchant for refined delicacies of Fifth Avenue like Pâte de foie gras and Maryland water turtle soup.
His menu that day was a little more modest: stuffed eggs in tomato sauce, mashed potatoes and, as dessert, a plum pudding.
"It was an act of culinary solidarity with suffering people," said Jane Ziegelman.
Her husband Andrew Coe added, "It was also a message to Americans about how they were supposed to eat."
The couple lives in Brooklyn Heights and are both food historians.
Coe's last book, "Chop Suey," was about Chinese cuisine in the U.S., while Ziegelman tells in her book "97 Orchard" about living in an apartment building on the Lower East Side from a food perspective.
Her latest collaboration, "A Decent Meal," which will be published by Harper Verlag on Tuesday, illustrates the history of American cuisine during the Great Depression.
They showed culinary solidarity as they meet with a reporter at Eisenberg's sandwich shop for dinner, a small, no-frills takeaway in the Flatiron District that has been in business since the stock market crash in 1929.
Ziegelman, 54, ordered a cream cheese sandwich with pieces of olive, while Coe, 57 ate turkey with mashed potatoes and vegetable mixture.
When the reporter ordered meage fries, the couple found this appropriate for a discussion of eating in the Great Depression era.
"Rich foods were very popular," Ziegelman said.
There was peanut bread, liver cheese and bean bread.
They were prepared from an ingredient and something cheap stretching the ingredient.
Imagine eating so many peanuts that they serve them as dinners.
Coe grinned.
"It must've settled in his stomach like lead," he said.
In the years before the Grand Depression, the table in an American household, especially in rural areas, was an all-you-can-eat buffet.
Teams of women cooked for male fieldworkers and freshly baked pâtÉs were served for breakfast, lunch and dinner.
It was the Americans who sent food to starving Europeans during World War II. "A Square Meal" records the ways with which the land dealt with no longer being the slarf country.
"It was a time when food became a central, tense topic for the Americans," Coe reasoned why he and his wife wanted to write about it.
Just as tense was the new budget diet: Mysterious dishes such as breads and casseroles became staples, the casserole as a "wonderful way to recycle leftovers," Ziegelman noted, as chefs could hide unappetizing ingredients under a creamy sauce.
Fresh fruit has been replaced by cheaper dried fruit.
Meat, which for years has been the focal point of American cuisine, has become a rarely served luxury.
Peculiar blends have been tried out as high-calorie and nourishing gratification took precedence over taste, or even healthy keeping.
During her research for the book, which also includes recipes, Ziegelman prepared a dish from that era, baked onions with peanut butter.
"It wasn't a popular addition at lunchtime," says Coe.
Ziegelmann continues: "It was surreal."
Peanut butter and baked onions don't match.
It was typical for many of housekeeping recipes.
As never before or ever since, housekeepers - including Louise Stanley, head of the Federal Bureau of Home Economics from 1923 to 1943 - drove the country's eating habits in such a way.
By publishing recipes and articles in newspapers and magazines, they encouraged women to become "household politicians" and live up to the challenge of turning pamp like creamy spaghetti with carrots into delicious meals.
"A Square Meal" is a celebration of historical delicacies.
In particular, the reports of the government's response to starving citizens, such as President Herbert Hoover's lass-mich-cake-eating attitude, are palatable.
While he publicly praised the simple Iowa farmhouse kitchen of his kitchen, he dined like the multimillion-dollar man of world to whom he has become.
"He liked continental cuisine like fish with cucumber sauce," Coe said.
He ate in an almost gilded dining room and wore a tuxedo.
He was gloriously unrealistic.
President Roosevelt might have made the same mistake if his wife Eleanor didn’t ask him to make an example with his stomach.
As described in the book, hiring a housekeeper with no interest in flavors by Ms. Roosevelt caused the White House to produce the most dull food in Washington, but also one of the most bleak prepared."
Many of the species from the Great Depression era to deal with food was discarded once the land got back on its feet.
The legacy is on our continued focus on calories and food, Ziegelman said, as well as on how science was applied to foods.
The couple indicated when writing the book the eating habits of Ziegelman's mother-in-law were better understood - for example, how she couldn't throw food away.
"She had a real fear of wasting food," Coe said.
Ziegelman recalled, "She actually made people sick."
That's when there's been the windbaag
After the plates at Eisenberg had cleared, they and Coe pulled out a dessert they had prepared at home: the plum dessert that President Roosevelt ate for lunch.
"It's called Prune Whip," Ziegelman said, adding that it was a common trick to miss out on modest recipes a fancy name.
There was no whipped cream, only plums, some flour, sugar, water and cinnamon.
Everyone dipped somewhat reluctantly a spoon into the twisty, brown glibber.
Surprisingly, it didn't taste bad.
Coe picked up three times.
Like a can-do budgeter, he said to his wife, "A few chopped walnuts would help."
Killed in bomb attack: He was Aleppo's hero
He put his own life on the line to help others: Khaled Omar.
He was the hero of Aleppo: Khaled Omar dedicated his life to the people of Syria.
He risked everything to save the lives of others.
Now the 30-year-old himself has died in a barrel bomb attack by the Assad regime.
Omar was a member of the "White Helmets", a civil protection group that has so far saved almost 60,000 lives, according to Bild-Zeitung.
2900 volunteers put their own lives at stake to draw people out of bombed-out homes and provide first aid in the war zone.
They are the heroes of war in Syria.
Omar was the face of the group: A video of the plainclothes protector showing him from the rubbles of a house during an 11-hour rescue of an infant went around the world in 2014.
It made the Syrian a shining symbol figure in the horror of war.
However, as a hero he has never seen himself, according to Bild said: "For me, this is real jihad."
“If I die to save lives, I think God sees a martyr in me.”
Shortly before Omar himself fell victim to an attack, he had spoken in a video about the last bombing raids in Syria.
Helicopters have dropped four barrel bombs over the Alzadia district, one was filled with chlorine gas.
One woman and two children died, and seven people suffer from shortness of breath.
"We are now eliminating the poisonous remains of the bomb so that children don't play with it," the picture quotes his last words before he died.
The deadly bomb of the Assad regime had been supposed to hit terrorists.
Grönland shark: The oldest vertebrate is almost 400 years old
Rare sight.
Grönland sharks usually only come to the surface in winter.
This shark was allowed to return to the sea after an investigation aboard the research vessel Sanna.
He was born in 1624 and swims through the North Atlantic at a good 1 km/h - until today.
Grönland sharks don't get offspring until 150 years old.
The Grönland hai floats leisurely along the bottom of the North Atlantic, in an hour it comes on average just 1,220 meters.
Only when it comes down to it, the five-meter-long animal accelerates to a whopping 2.6 km/h - and, with this top speed, is half as fast as a seal.
However, this slow lifestyle seems to be a recipe for old age.
Julius Nielsen from the University of Copenhagen and his colleagues report in the journal "Science" in any case about a Greenland shark, which could be 392 years old.
"Insanity," comments Alexander Scheuerlein of the Max Planck Institute for Demographic Research in Rostock on this life span, which represents a world record for vertebrates.
The evolutionary biologist has long suspected that Greenland sharks are getting stone-aged.
Only the evidence was missing.
Grönland sharks are difficult to observe, after all, they live mainly in the ice sea off the sparsely populated coasts of Canada, Greenland, Norway and Spitsbergen.
Because they prefer water temperatures around six degrees Celsius, they usually only appear on the surface in winter.
Accordingly, researchers know little about the lifestyle of Grönland sharks.
They measured their slow-motion speed as they equipped six animals with a transmitter in front of Spitsbergen.
These sharks make prey anyway: In the stomach of an animal, researchers found the remains of a seal, another had swallowed the jawbone of a young polar bear.
Possibly they're chasing or patrolling at the bottom of the ice sea, seeking carcasses stumbling down from higher layers of water.
Julius Nielsen and his colleagues have now examined 28 Greenland sharks, which fishermen retrieved between 2010 and 2013 before Greenland or Spitsbergen as a bycatch from the depths of the North Atlantic.
The smallest shark was just 81 centimeters long, while the tallest 502 centimetres measured.
To determine the age of the animals, they used the carbon-14 method to analyze the shark's lens core.
This center of the eye lens arises even before the birth of the sharks, the proteins contained therein are no longer renewed later.
The two smallest sharks had a striking amount of carbon-14 in the lentil core.
Their birth should therefore lie after the early 1960s, when nuclear weapons tests hurled larger amounts of this radioactive carbon into the atmosphere.
In the third-smallest animal with a length of 220 centimeters, on the other hand, the lens nucleus contained a small excess of carbon-14 from the nuclear weapons tests, so it may have celebrated its 50th birthday during its investigation in 2012.
All larger Greenland sharks, on the other hand, had normal amounts of carbon-14 in their lens nucleus, which is produced by cosmic radiation in the atmosphere.
In fact, the carbon-14 clock for a 493-centimeter-long Greenland shark showed an age of 335 years, while the record shark with 502 centimeters is likely to be 392 years old.
It was known that Greenland shark females only get offspring when they are longer than four meters.
Now the researchers were able to assign an age to the length.
Since one of the animals with 392 centimeters was probably on the threshold of sexual maturity and his age was determined at 156 years, Grönland sharks with the propagation obviously take at least one and a half centuries.
This, too, is likely to be a world record for vertebrates at present.
That such methusalemes live mainly in icy waters is no coincidence.
The previous record holder, a 211-year-old Grönland whale in whose bacon a 120-year-old harpoon tip was stuck on the neck, was also caught off Alaska.
Due to the low temperatures, the organism of the animals living there often also runs on saving flame.
So they mature later - and age more slowly.
In order to keep warm in the cold water, the animals often become particularly large.
Because the volume grows much faster than its surface through which the animal loses heat.
The heat loss of one gram of mouse is therefore much greater than with a gram of elephant.
Another advantage is that you have fewer enemies.
"A smaller animal lives more dangerously and should focus on having as many descendants as possible as early as possible," explains the evolutionary biologist Scheuerlein.
A very large animal, on the other hand, should rather invest its resources in a long life.
Subsidue in double pack
In the most recent meeting, the municipal council focused on topics relating to construction.
With relief, the panel included Mayor Robert Putz's notice that "finally the marketing of the most recently designated residential areas can begin."
After the land, parcelling and development issues could be clarified and the applications for survey could be made, nothing stands in the way of the sale of the land in the residential areas "Straßlweg" and "Schönau-West" (area Binderstraße - extension section 1) to interested parties.
The municipal council is convinced that the building areas in the rural and quiet residential community of Schönau appeal to young families and sprightly senior citizens alike.
As it was said, construction volunteers can now report to the town hall, about the allocation of the plots the local council is expected to discuss in its September meeting.
In order to be able to respond to the establishment of further businesses, combined with the creation of jobs, the local council decided to amend the land use plan, which had only been re-established in the spring of this year.
Thus, in the area Unterzeitlarn, the commercial area already designated there will be extended by two plots in the direction of Staatsstraße 2112.
Frankfurt benefits from Brexit
In competition with Paris, Dublin and Luxembourg, Stadt am Main has the best chance of attracting relocated jobs and companies from London.
"It is very likely that Frankfurt will benefit the most from the Brexit vote," says Michael Voigtländer, head of IW's financial markets and real estate markets.
According to the Brexit referendum, it is uncertain whether money houses will be allowed to continue to conduct financial transactions from London across the EU.
For the so-called EU passport so far, their approval in the United Kingdom is sufficient.
That's what big U.S. banks are taking advantage of right now.
Several banks have already announced plans to relocate jobs.
Frankfurt Main Finance is expecting 10000 new jobs on the Main within five years.
According to the IW, the headquarters of the European Central Bank (ECB), which brings together responsibility for monetary policy and oversight over the largest banks in the eurozone, as well as other important authorities, such as the European insurance supervisory authority EIOPA and the ESRB Risk Council, speak for Frankfurt.
The Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development (OECD) is headquartered in Paris, but this is less important for the financial sector than the ECB.
"Proximity to the central bank is important for banks, as they can thus communicate directly with the supervisory authority," says IW professor Voigtländer.
Luxembourg is home to a large fund industry, but, like Dublin, scores worse in terms of infrastructure.
Thanks to the large airport, Frankfurt is much easier to reach.
In addition, the city scores with easily available commercial properties.
"It's not so easy to find free high-quality offices in Paris," says Voigtländer.
In addition, rents for first-class commercial real estate are 50 percent higher than on the Maine.
In addition, Frankfurt offers a higher quality of life than the competition thanks to good medical care and infrastructure as well as less crime.
In a ranking of the management consultancy Mercer, the city ranks seventh, Luxembourg in 19th place, Dublin at number 33 and Paris in 37th place.
Even if quality of life is a question of preferences: "With its already strong position as a financial location for the European single market, Frankfurt could attract further banks and investors," the IW concludes.
London, however, will remain the most important financial center in Europe despite the Brexit vote, the economic researchers say.
According to figures from Landesbank Helaba at the end of 2015, around 144000 people worked in the banking industry in London at the end of 2015, compared to 62500 in Frankfurt.
Gilead receives legal costs from hepatitis C patent dispute with Merck
A U.S. magistrate ruled that Gilead Sciences Inc is entitled to the reimbursement of the legal costs it incurred from the Hepatits C litigation with the pharmaceutical company Merck & Co Inc.
In June, Gilead was freed from a $200 million in damages payment for Merck's infringement of two patents related to blockbuster drugs Sovaldi and Harvoni of Gilead after a U.S. judge uncovered a pattern of misconduct on the part of Mercks, including perjury and other unethical acts.
In filing the lawsuit on Thursday, U.S. magistrate Beth Labson Freeman said Gilead is entitled to waiver the costs incurred while defending the case.
Merck is trying to match with Gilead, which dominates the market of a new generation of hepatitis C drugs, which can cure over 90 percent of patients with liver disease.
The case dates back to 2013, when Gilead and Merck sued each other and claimed property on laboratory work underempanied by Sofosbuvir, the active ingredient in Gilead's drugs.
U.S.-backed troops say they've regained complete control of Manbij from the Islamic State
U.S.-backed troops have recaptured complete control of the northern city of Manbij, near the Turkish border, after the last IS fighters in the city leave it, a spokesman for the group said Friday.
Syria's (DKS) Democratic Forces are currently scouring the city following the exodus of the still-remaining group of fighters buried inside the city.
