"The city is under complete control now, but we're carrying out clean-up right now," he told Reuters.
Clintens earn 10.75 million in 2015 and pay 34.2% federal taxes
Hillary Clinton released her 2015 tax return on Friday, yielding an income of USD 10.75 million and an effective federal tax rate of 34.2% for Democrat presidential nominee and her husband.
In 2015, the Clintons donated USD 1 million to charitable causes, mainly to the Clinton Foundation; former President Bill Clinton generated nearly USD 5.3 million in speech fees; the former secretary of state gave an income of USD 3 million from publisher Simon & Schuster for her book on her tenure at the State Department.
Clinton's nominee for the vice presidency, Senator Tim Kaine of Virginia, together with his wife Anne Holton, released his tax returns of the past ten years.
They paid an effective federal tax rate of 20.3% in 2015.
"Hillary Clinton and Tim Kaine continue to set standards in terms of financial transparency," Clinton's campaign aide Jennifer Palmieri said in a statement.
In a blatant contrast, Donald Trump hides behind false excuses and pull-backs in relation to his previous promises to release his tax returns.
It's common for U.S. presidential nominees to release their tax returns even though they're not required to do so by law.
Trump, a New York businessman, and his lawyers have cited a tax audit by the tax authorities as the reason for his denial.
"You're on the move," Clinton's campaign aider Ian Sams wrote on Twitter, referring to the release of Democrats' presidential nominee.
On Thursday, Michael Cohen, Trump's special adviser, told CNN that he wouldn't allow Trump to release anything before the tax audits aren't completed.
Trump's critics, including the Republican presidential nominee of 2012 Mitt Romney, have already announced that his refusal raises questions regarding assets, his donations to charitable organizations, his business conduct and various other connections, particularly with Russia.
Clinton has already overthrown himself on the issue and released a video on the Internet on Friday in which high-ranking Republicans urge Trump to release his tax return.
On Thursday, she also raised the issue during a speech on the economy in Michigan.
"He refuses to do what any other presidential nominee has done over the past few decades and to release his tax returns," she told the crowd.
Politico has reported that Trump paid none or only very little in taxes for two years during the 1990s, and a New York Times business journalist cited a number of tax lawyers and tax advisers on Friday who indicated that could still be the case.
Federal taxes have become an issue during the election campaign.
Clinton supports a scheme named after billionaire and investor Warren Buffett, which would ensure that people who earn more than USD 1 million pay a tax rate of at least 30%.
President Obama also supports the proposal.
During a campaign event for Hillary Clinton last week in Omaha, Nebraska demanded Buffett, whose Berkshire Hathaway industrial conglomerate has its seat here, to exchange Trump for a meeting around their tax returns.
Buffett said he had also been subjected to an IRS tax audit and Trump was "fearful" of voters rather than tax authorities.
Clinton's campaign has received task returns by 2007.
The Clintons, who are now living in Chappaqua, New York, paid, on averaging, an effective federal tax rate of about 32% between the years 2007 to 2014 and an effective combined tax rate of about 40.5%.
The Clintons' 2015 tax return showed that they only received USD 100 of their income as wages, unlike the bulk of all Americans.
Their main investment was a cost-effective index fund from which the Clinton's dividends and yields of USD 109,000 taxed.
