Chili powder condoms, firecrancers promote Tanzania's elephant protection
Conservationists in Tanzania use an unorthodox species to stop elephants from going into man-occupied settlements - they throw condoms at them filled with chilli powders.
The method has proven to be effective and the Honeyguide Foundation, which already had the idea years ago, has intensified its promotion together with the U.S. nature conservation by training volunteers in villages in northern Tanzania to use a non-violent four-stage method to protect their homes and crops without violating animals.
Earlier, a lot of spears used to defend themselves.
Friday is World Elephant Day dedicated to animal welfare.
"Sincence we've been developing the ... Toolkit, we've been able to observe a change in their attitude within these communities, being much more confident that they can keep the elephants from their fields without harming the elephant in doing so," Honeyguide Damian Bell's chief executive said in a statement.
The first two steps include illuminating the elephant with a torch and blowing into a horn.
If he isn't deterred, a so-called "chili cloud" is thrown.
Chili powder mixed with earth is packed together with a firecracker into a condom, the end of which is twisted down to the ignition cord.
When it is lit, the condom bursts open with a bang and sprays fine dust on chilli powders into the air.
Quite a hint is usually ready to chase away the elephant.
One last possibility is a thrower that shoots into the air a golden rainfire, causing a noisy, bright explosion in the process.
