Claude Lanzmann, author of the Holocaust documentary "Shoah," left out his annoyance at Kempinski Bristol Hotel in Berlin by writing an open letter to German newspaper FAZ.
During his last stay, Lanzmann tried to find Israel on the hotel's pre-election
However, the country was not listed between the countries that can be called directly from the hotel room.
How is it possible in 2016 in Berlin, the capital of the new Germany, that Israel has been eliminated and deleted?
Lanzmann wrote.
Instead, Kempinksi Bristol offers a way to select Israel via its own call center.
Looking for an explanation, the director approached the hotel reception.
The response he received there "shocked him," as Lanzmann wrote.
The hotel employee said the measure was a "deliberate decision made by Kempinski Hotels management."
The reason behind it sparked even more anger in the director.
"The majority of our guests are Arabs and they have demanded that the pre-election be deleted," Lanzmann's employee was quoted as saying.
Israel's ambassador to Germany Yakov Hadas-Handelsman called this a "great disgrace," the Süddeutsche Zeitung reports.
We were horrified and dismayed by this event.
That's really very shameful.
The fact that this has happened in Germany in exactly this hotel chain is even greater shame.
It doesn't require an explanation.
We expect the hotel "to draw the right conclusions," the official said.
The incident sparked a rather great response on the Internet where people called the whole thing a scandal.
Some insinuated to the Kempinski Hotel that it had "given up" the alleged demands of Arab guests.
"Shame on you," it said in another post, pointing out that the hotel, which is located on the poshible Kurfürstendamm, is located near a synagogue.
The hotel quickly dismissed all the accusations and called the incident an "accident" and "apologized" at Lanzmann, Der Spiegel reports.
"There has never been a direct instruction" not to include Israel in the earal dial list," the Süddeutsche Zeitung quoted a spokesperson for Kempinski Hotel as saying.
It adds that the country code has now been added.
Police use tear gas & rubber bullets against protesting Brazilian students
Students clashed with police on Thursday in the Brazilian city of Sao Paulo as they rallied to demonstrate against the government's reforms for the public education system.
Police used tear gas and rubber bullets against the protesters.
The difficulties began when police officers tried to drive the students apart, causing police to deploy rubber bullets and tear gas.
Hundreds of students took part in the demonstrations to express their dismay over the plans.
According to proposals from the governor of the state of Sao Paulo Geraldo Alckmin, slightly less than 100 public schools are being closed, while 300,000 students are being implemented to save money.
Some of the protest participants saw graffiti spray-painting on walls against the provisional presidential Michel Temer.
The latest protests occurred a few days after demonstrators took to the streets in crowds to support suspended President Dilma Rousseff, who had been removed from office by vote of the Senate on her dismissal.
Trade between Russia & Iran suddenly rises to 71% - RT Business
With the lifting of the sanctuaries, according to Russia's ambassador to Iran, Levan Dzhagaryan, trade turnover between Moscow and Tehran grew 70.9 percent year-on-year.
Since international sanctions against Iran were lifted, interest on the part of Russian companies in business in the country has grown sharply, the ambassador said.
Russian companies accrue existing ties with Iran, while new firms are ready to gain access to the market.
Of course, further trade expansion is one of the biggest priorities of bilateral cooperation with Iran," Dzhagaryan said.
He noted that trade turnover between Russia and Iran increased by 70.9 percent in the first five months of 2016 compared to the same period in 2015, amounting to $856 million.
In particular, Russian imports into Iran skyrocketed by 91.5 percent, amounting to $697 million.
Russian imports from Iran rose 16 percent to $158 million.
The trade upturn has been achieved through deliveries of machinery, land vehicles and weapons," the ambassador said.
Dzhagaryan expects a record number of Iranian tourists in Russia for this year.
Last year, Russia's diplomatic mission issued around 35,000 visas, primarily travel visas, to Iranian citizens.
In addition, Iran reached the top 20 of the countries when it comes to the number of tourists in Russia.
You could say that the record numbers of Iranian tourists in Russia from last year will be broken," he said.
At the same time, relatively few Russians are visiting Iran, something the ambassador hopes will change.
"Personally, I can recommend visiting the cities of Isfahan, Shiraz and Yazd, which have received monuments of ancient Persian civilization in good state," he said.
Witnesses wanted: Beverage market in Waldbröl ambushed
A robbery on Thursday evening was committed at the beverage market of Rewe XXL on the Waldbröler Gerberstraße.
While holding a firearm, three darkly dressed people, who had hooded their faces, demanded from the employee of the beverage market the surrender of cash and then fled first on foot and then presumably with a silver-colored VW golf.
How much money has been looted, the police does not disclose.
The perpetrators are said to have been between 1.60 and 1.70 meters tall, so relatively small.
The beverage market is accessible directly from the parking deck of the Rewe market and is difficult to view.
The perpetrators must have run after the robbery in the direction of Gerberstraße, Friedrich-Engelbert-Weg and fled from the gravel parking lot opposite the parking garage entrance with the silver-coloured car, possibly in the direction of B 256 (Schladernring).
Anyone who made observations at the time of the crime (around 19:45) in connection with the robbery should urgently contact the police.
Nagold: Play and have fun with summer leisure
The children and adolescents of the social-educational daily/social group work of the children’s protection association Nagold spent eight beautiful and happy days with their carers on their summer leisure in Nussdorf, near Mühlacker.
In the quietly located leisure home Hardtwäldle, the fun-loving group was accommodated during the first week of school holidays.
Several all-day excursions were on the program.
On the one hand, we went to Bretten with a visit to the petting zoo.
On the other hand to Maulbronn, where there was a special children's tour in the world-famous monastery.
The third trip led to Pforzheim.
At the Celtic Museum in Hochdorf, the group learned a lot about everyday life and the food of the Celts.
On the hot days, the group enjoyed themselves at Tiefensee in Maulbronn, which invited to jump, swim and splash.
Culinary, children and caregivers were spoiled with their self-prepared favorite dishes such as the Sauren Spätzle, the Seebronner Pfännle, Iselshauser-Bloff, the English-Breakfast and Tübinger Bolognese.
In workshops it was beatified, beautiful jewelry made of pearls and stones or made of wood a Roman mill game.
Other highlights of free time were fishing in a nearby pond, the evenings by the fire and the concluding "Colourful Evening."
On the day of the journey home, the sun laughed and the children and youngsters returned home satisfied.
Haigerloch: Pilgrimages to the Gate of Mercy
The ministers from the pastoral unit Eyachtal-Haigerloch St. Anna were on their way on a four-day pilgrimage from Villingen to Freiburg.
Four stages were tackled together.
In order to bridge the time until the next Rome pilgrimage of all ministrants in probably two years, the Prime Ministrant Round St. Anna has decided to prepare and carry out a multi-day pilgrimage.
Various pilgrimage destinations were examined and it was finally decided for the episcopal city of Freiburg, where this year, on the occasion of the Holy Year of Mercy, a special pilgrimage destination was offered with the gate of Mercy opened at the Freiburg Cathedral.
16 minis from Bittelbronn, Gruol, Haigerloch and Heiligenzimmern set out.
The night before Father Storost sent out the pilgrims in a small celebration in St. Anna, blessed the Pilgrimage crosses and granted the pilgrim's blessing.
From Horb was driven by train to the starting point Villingen, where there is also a Cathedral of Our Lady and so the Pilgrimage Route from Münster was started.
In the morning and evening, the individual daily tours were framed by a short impulse with various prayers, songs and writings.
But also on the way, the minis at field crosses, chapels or churches again and again to sing a song together made of specially compiled Pilger-Liedheft.
For the overnight stays, parish homes of the local parishes were available.
Alexandra Burkhardt and Heidi Faller provided a tasty as well as strengthening dinner and breakfast at the stage destinations.
On the second day we went via Furtwangen to Gütenbach.
The next day, they started the stage to St. Peter, which was the most stressful route, but had to be climbed around 500 vertical meters in sultry heat and then climbed again.
The deserved reward in the evening was a joint barbecue at the Badweiher.
After the morning impulse on the fourth day of pilgrimage in the imposing monastery church of St. Peter we went on the final stage to Freiburg.
Visibly relieved and happily, the Minis arrived at Freiburg's Minstern later in the afternoon and crossed the gate of mercy.
With a little impetus, God was thanked here.
In the evening Mass in Münster, the minis arrived at the Pilgrim destination were greeted by co-operator Markus Obert.
After the service, the pilgrims were able to visit the sacristy and the high altar.
The next day there was still time for Breisgau metropolis.
In the afternoon we went by train back to Horb.
In the end, all participants agreed that the days were marked by spirituality and a strong community experience.
Scot Callum Skinner storms home as Brite wins gold for cycling
Scotland's Callum Skinner raced to fame last night in the men's team sprint at Velodrome in Rio as the British team defended London 2012 title.
The 23-year-old from Glasgow took the anchor role his compatriot Sir Chris Hoy had embraced. The youngster made the legend proud as he stormed home to earn a victory from favourites New Zealand.
England claimed a shocking victory four years ago, while Hoy clinched the fifth of six Olympic gold medals.
They repeated the trick after picking up only a modest sixth-place finish at the World Track Championships in London in March despite Hoy's resignation.
The problem between Beijing and London was the replacement of driver Jamie Staff.
They did this in time when Hindes appeared.
Hoy's retirement created a gap Kian Emadi and Matt Crampton tried to close.
Skinner was the man they stayed with when they saw potential in the Scot, which has now unfolded.
Hindes, Kenny - who won with Hoy four years ago - and Skinner set an Olympic record in the qualifying rounds, but the New Zealanders prevailed in the first lap.
And the British trio won the duel with gold.
Hindes was narrowly behind after his lap, but Kenny brought the team forward and Skinner held it.
A change of strategy accounted for a large part of the transformation in which Hindes drove in a higher gear to slow it down, allowing Kenny and, above all, Skinner to keep pace.
Hoy watched from his position as a BBC pundit and stood up to Skinner, Kenny and Hindes as these climbed onto the podium.
The 23-year-old Skinner from Glasgow said: "It's a flattering comparison."
I'm only here to do my best and make a name for myself.
We set an Olympic record in the finals and defeated the world champions.
It's just been unbelievable.
There is no better way to win this.
After triumph in 2012 alongside Kennys and Hoys, Hindes admitted that he deliberately fell on the track after a weak start.
He later withdrew that statement and there was no objection from England's rivals.
There was no such a problem this time with such an outstanding victory.
Hindes said: "It feels with the whole GB flags in the stands like London."
We've been coming together as a team over the last three months.
We've come away with so many world titles so bad that it was unbelievable to win again at the Olympics.
That's an event, a gold medal for the nation won seven out of 10 titles in both Beijing and London.
If that’s a barometer of our progress over the last five months, we’re looking forward to successful five days in velodrome.
With Form Kennys, it's in the realm of possibility that he'll repeat Hoy's victory of three Olympic gold medals in a contest.
That, for Kenny - who's exactly 12 years younger than Hoy, who was also born on March 23 (an apparently holy date for British sport; on that day Steve Redgrave, Mo Farah and Roger Bannister were also born) - that would mean a yield of six gold medals and a silver medal.
That'd be the same from Hoy.
Kenny next takes part in the sprint, which begins today and will go over three days.
The 28-year-old from Bolton said, "I'm enjoying this just for the moment."
The team event is always best.
You can win it with your colleagues.
It feels a bit lonely to win on your own.
Hindes said, "If he wins two more gold medals, he should be knighted."
It was the only medal competition of the day, but there was more good news for England.
Sir Bradly Wiggins, Ed Clancy, Steven Burke and Owain Doull qualified with the fastest performance in the four-man four-kilometer race.
They arrived at the finish in 3:51,942.
The world record, which won gold in London in 2012, stood at 3:51.659.
The first and final round (22.42) will take place today on the second day of the competition, in which Wiggins will certainly be in focus.
He had been arguing with appointees before qualifying yesterday, when functionaries shouted at him to measure his body size.
In the corresponding women's competition, which will be resumed and finished tomorrow, Laura Trott, Joanna Rowsell Shand, Elinor Barker and Katie Archibald of Milngavie advanced in world record time in 4:13.260.
The GB team relies on its strong track cycling team to dominate the middle period of the Olympics and pick up a bunch of medals.
After a truly extraordinary depiction of absolute dominance in Beijing and London, the expectations for Rio were reduced.
Dramas took place in the background, according to the resignation of technical director Shane Sutton in April amid sexism and bullying allegations, as well as the retirement of greats like Hoy and Victoria Pendleton.
However, if you look at the first evening of the competition in velodrome, it looks quite like the now legendary reputation of GB, which has reached its culmination at the perfect moment, could be repeated again.
Future of Portland smeltery due to cancellation of energy contract questionable
The future of Portland's aluminium meltdown in Victoria - as well as hundreds of workers' jobs - are at stake, as the plant operator will terminate the energy supply contract and thus cancel all supply contracts, the supplying energy company said.
This move followed the government's decision in Victoria earlier this year not to allocate any further subsidies to operate the plant.
A subsidy set for 20 years expires in November.
Up to 2000 jobs at Alcoa's plant in Portland are at stake.
No fewer than 2000 jobs - direct as well as indirect - could be at risk in the event of closure.
In addition, there could be domino effects, as the smeltery consumes around 10 percent of Victoria's total electricity generation.
In 2014, Alcoa closed the Point Henry smeltery, which led to a closure of the Anglesea power plant last year.
Alcoa's smeltery in Portland loses money and risks closure.
This move follows years of speculation regarding the future of the plant, originally as a result of the strong Australian dollar and most recently due to the electricity price for the plant.
AGL, which supplies the large aluminum smelting plant with its Loy Yang A power plant in Latrobe Valley, said it expects three more owners to terminate their contracts.
The plant is majority-owned in a joint venture of Alcoa and Alumina, of which the Chinese company CITIC and Japan's Marubeni Corp hold smaller shares.
"AGL expects the Portland plant to continue to operate, which means further physical demand for electricity through the smelter," the company said in a statement.
AGL further stated that the contracts were so-called "fusing transactions" that should have come into force from November 2016.
The contracts have a 12-month notice period, so they will end in 2017.
"Therefore, AGL assumes that the contracts will contribute to the revenues in fiscal year 17," the electricity supplier said.
All potential impact on revenues on AGL as of GJ 18 is currently limited by the forecasts of the strong futures markets for wholesale electricity prices.
AGL said it had already depreciated the value of the contracts in its end-of-year, amounting to $187 million, in "expectation of termination."
Alcoa said that the decision to withdraw the contracts did not affect the "Ability of Alcoa... the smeltery or to ensure the power supply to the smeltery".
The U.S. company said it had signed a 20-year contract with Loy Yang in 2010, which should have gone into effect from November this year, even if it still owes a statement as to why it resigned from the contract.
Analysts warn of the possible closure of Portland smeltery in Victoria and the Tomago facility near Newcastle due to poor economic conditions of the industry.
Referring to analysts' statements earlier this week, Head of Operation Stephen Mikkelsen said Portland consume around 600 megawatts of electricity each year and Tomago.
If both plants disappear from the market, this would mean a significant reduction in demand in the [electricity market].
And for us as power generators, that wouldn't be good," he said.
More than just a Kardashian reality TV star, Kendall is on the cover of the September issue of Vogue
Kendall Jenner, 20, model and fourth daughter of the manager and mother of Kardashian-Jenner Clan Kris is on the cover of the coveted September edition of US Vogue.
Kendall, who is Estee Lauder's brand ambassador and ran at Fashion Weeks in Milan, Paris and New York, announced the news on her Instagram account on Thursday.
"In a room full of people I love, the sight of this cover made me cry," Kendall wrote.
The unveiling of the cover was followed by a bizarre (or simply Keeping Up with the Kardashians similar?), scripted reality video showing the entire Kardashian-Jenner family (with the exception of problem cases Rob and Caitlyn, as well as in the presence of the most popular showbiz uncle and producer of Keeping Up Ryan Seacrest), as they all have planned the cover of their sister and which ends with a classic twist
That represents the clichéd plot of almost every birthday episode of a children's show (Seacrest brought the sentence, "You thought that we had forgotten you?" with the honesty of a cartoon rabbit on a broadcasing site at 8 a.m.), but Kris somehow had her other girls take part in this publicity.
With regard to the September issue of Vogue, a new era of Kardashians has certainly begun.
Kendall's big sister Kim may have beaten her as far as Vogue's cover is concerned (she and her husband Kanye West were on display in April 2014), but the cover of the magazine's September issue is a league unto itself.
For Vogue, the September edition is the most selling edition of the year.
With a perimeter that resembles a picture book rather than a fashion magazine, the issue attracts a large number of advertisers (according to Fashionista, 615 pages were advertising from the 832 pages of the Wälzer).
The unveiling of his cover star is so eagerly awaited that in the last two years, pictures of the magazine leaked even before the official announcement.
Women who were already on the cover include Naomi Campbell, Linda Evangelista and Kate Moss.
In recent years, clearly more "celebrities modeling" than "models who are celebrities" have been on the cover.
Oscar-winner and Dior brand ambassador Jennifer Lawrence was on the cover in 2013, a year after Lady Gaga had the honor.
But even with the low chance that Ms Jenner - described by the magazine as her generation's "breakthrough model" - falls under the earlier category, she finds herself in good company.
These celebrities aren’t related to famous people, or reality stars or teenagers of today, who know how to shoot a good Snapchat video (even if it’s all true for Jenner).
They're really talented women excelling in the entertainment industry.
Beyonce, who appeared on the last cover, had one of the most successful years of her career with the release of Lemonade.
Of course Jenner's success doesn't please everyone.
With her reality TV history, Jenner's position in the fashion world has always been somewhat tense.
In June, the one-time Victoria's Secret model Stephanie Seymour Jenner and her colleague Gigi Hadid criticised her, calling her "the bitches of the moment."
Those feelings boiled over again when the news came out with Jenner's appearance on the Vogue cover.
But riding on the displeasure seems to pass by the point.
Kendall Jenner is on the cover of Vogue's September issue.
She's literally a supermodel.
She's running for Marc Jacobs.
She's advertising your priming by Estee Lauder.
She and all her sisters sell you pictures of yourself on their respective paywall apps.
She sells pictures of Cindy Crawford's daughter made by her to LOVE magazine.
And in her spare time she can be seen on reality TV. Probably just to see her sister Kourtney and their children.
Community law centres expel 160,000 people annually
More than 160,000 of the country's aid needs are turned away annually by community law centres, while the industry is prepared for the impact of further fund cuts on already overburdened transactions.
In Queensland, three out of five people - mostly elderly people, immigrants, workers in need of help, victims of acts of violence, and those who have no funds for unequitable delogations and the same - are dismissed in their request for free legal advice and legislature.
Legal services of the municipality, which are already unduly utilised, are ready for further cuts.
From June next year it is envisaged that these legal centers will lose another 30 percent of their federal funds.
Queesland's director James Farrell's director said that the last survey by the National Association of Community Rights Centers found that thousands of people are already turned away every year.
Farrell said that the situation would only get worse without a repeal of the federal government's decision.
"Queensland's community rights centers are chronically underfunded, which means that thousands of people are turned away by important services you need," he said.
People who leave violent relationships, people with unmanageable and crushing debts, families going through break-ups, workers who have been unjustly laid off - all of these people have problems of a legal nature and this elevation shows that they can't get the help they need.
The federal government will cut funding by 30 per century from June 2017, which means that thousands of people in need from Queensland will be forced to cope with their legal problems without any help.
We call on the Attorney General and Federal Justice Minister, as well as senators of Queensland George Brandis, to lift these funding cuts immediately so that people can receive the help they need on legal issues.
Last year, the state government fulfilled its commitment to raise funds for legal aid to the national average.
It is now time to look at the needs of the community rights centers and their client communities, as well as that the Palaszczuk government is investing in this important work.
Donald Trump election campaign indicted after employees allegedly drew gun
New York: Donald Trump's campaign has been charged with alleged inaccuracy after she was told that her campaign manager for North Carolina was targeting a firearm at an employee in February while traveling in a Jeep.
The incident is described in an indictment filed by Vincent Bordini, a former software programmer for the campaign in that state on Wednesday in Charlotte state tribunal.
Bordini claimed to have waited for months that the matter would be settled internally before he realised that "that wouldn't happen."
The campaign leader, Earl Philip, "pulled a pistol, put his right index finger on the trigger, and rammed the barrel into Vincent's knuckle," Bordini indicated in his indictment.
After complaining to the local and regional campaign leadership, Bordini found that at least four other people had already had the same experience with Phillip, he stated in the indictment.
"Weapons don't have to be fired to cause damage," Bordini said.
The indictment comes just two days after Republican presidential nominee sparked turmoil when he told a crowd in North Carolina that the "Second Negation People" - firearms owners and gun friends - should take action in case his rivinal Hillary Clinton is elected and should appoint judges.
Trump's campaign clarified that the mediaries had taken his words out of context and that he wouldn't incite violence.
Bordini's complaint seeks damages from the campaign for breach of due diligence and from Philip for personal injury and permanent mental damage.
Trump's press secretary Hope Hicks couldn't be reached for comment.
Attempts to contact Philip also failed.
WCCB, a local news channel in Charlotte, reports that Phillip is no longer an employee of the campaign.
Calls from his consulting company, Innovative Consulting Services, were not responded to.
Republican presidential nominee Donald Trump has run a controversial campaign so far.
The proceeding is Bordini v. Donald J. Trump For President Inc., 16-CS-14300, Supreme Court, County of Mecklenburg (Charlotte).
Berlin/Schwerin: Poll before state elections: SPD and CDU lose
In the upcoming elections in the federal states of Mecklenburg-Vorpommern (4 September) and Berlin (September 18), significant losses for the ruling parties SPD and CDU are emerging.
This was shown by representative surveys by the opinion research institute INSA for the "Bild" newspaper (Friday).
While the red-and-black ruling coalition in the north could narrowly continue to govern, according to the polling figures in Berlin, this is not possible.
In both federal states, however, the SPD would be the strongest party and would thus have a good chance of continuing to place the head of government.
In Mecklenburg-Vorpommern, the SPD crashed from 35.6 percent five years ago to 24 percent.
According to the poll, the party would be only slightly ahead of the CDU, which could come back to 23 percent.
Third place is controversial between the left and the AfD.
The left would come to 19 percent (previously 18.4 percent), the AfD would be represented with 19 percent for the first time in the state parliament.
The Greens, despite slight losses with 6 percent (previously 8.7 percent), rejoin the Schwerin state parliament.
NPD and FDP would not be represented in the state parliament with 3 percent each.
In the Berlin parliamentary elections, the SPD according to the poll would only come to 23 percent (election 2011: 28.3 percent).
The CDU reached only 18 percent after 23.4 percent.
It would still be behind the Greens with 19 percent (2011: 17.6 percent), but before the left with 15 percent (previously 11.7 percent).
The AfD would be represented in the House of Representatives with 14 percent for the first time.
With 5 percent, the FDP could hope for a returnance, the pirates, on the other hand, in contrast to 2011, would miss the entry.
Nokia 9000 Communicator: The first smartphone
E-mail and internet in slow motion: 20 years ago the Nokia Communicator came on the market.
The successors dominate the world today - its manufacturer experienced a disaster.
The device weighed almost half a kilogram and was more than four times as heavy as an iPhone 5.
It was thicker and bigger, so it wasn’t good in your hand.
On the contrary, it was pretty chunky.
The built-in memory initially amounted to eight megabytes, which today would be enough for about two high-resolution selfies.
An iPhone now comes with at least 16 gigabytes.
The "Nokia 9000 Communicator" entered the shops exactly 20 years ago, on August 15, 1996 - and it was the first smartphone.
For a few euros, lovers can still buy the devices on the Internet, but they somehow come from the Stone Age.
20 years ago, however, the communicator was a sensation.
The Finnish mobile phone manufacturer had announced the product a few months earlier at the Cebit computer fair in Hanover as an "office in vest pocket format".
In fact, the new mobile phone coulded a lot for standards at that time: SMS and e-mails could be sent and received, also by fax the communicator could be reached.
Even access to web pages was possible via HTML browser, albeit excruciatingly slow.
A calculator was available, a digital address book and an electronic calendar.
There was a small antenna, and the cell phone could be unfolded similarly to a laptop.
Then there was a small keyboard and a black-and-white screen.
The price was quite proud: 2700 D-Mark, i.e. about 1400 euros.
The average selling price of a smartphone in Germany was 392 euros in 2015.
No one could have guessed in August 1996 that the communicator would become the start of the smartphone era.
Everything's been different ever since.
The smart phones are a success story, though not for Nokia.
The mobile phone division of the Finns experienced an unprecedented disaster, was eventually sold and unwound to Microsoft.
The market is now dominated by providers, all of whom came much later than Nokia
Being online always and everywhere, that’s everyday life for a lot of people today.
In 2015, there were almost two billion smartphone users worldwide.
About 1.4 billion devices were solded, Samsung and Apple alone accounted for 555 million units.
The worldwide turnover with the devices is around 400 billion euros.
In addition, there are the well-runing transactions of the countless app providers and the telecommunications companies.
An entire industry has now emerged around the smartphone.
The decisive breakthrough to the mass market only came in early 2007, when Apple CEO Steve Jobs announced the introduction of the iPhone at Mac-World.
He promised three devices in one: a music player, a phone and an internet communicator, everything via touch screen easy to use.
Apple's triumphal procession began, even today the iPhone is the company's most important product.
Nokia, Motorola and Blackberry, which dominated the market, were left behind.
They eventually disappeared, Blackberry struggled.
Apple and Asian vendors such as Samsan Samsung are now in charge.
The smartphone is a matter of course 20 years after its invention.
On average, every German now looks at his mobile phone 88 times a day.
"We all have to learn to put the smartphone away," said Janina Kugel, responsible for personnel on the Siemens board.
Permanent accessibility is now regarded as a curse and a blessing at the same time.
England needs to steer the Olympic spirit and expand Heathrow
Heathrow will bring renewal and prosperity not only in our local municipalities and throughout London, but across the country.
For me, this represented Heathrow's biggest attraction, just as it did in London back in 2012, when we put a lot of effort into ensuring that all our nations and regions benefited from the games.
The Olympics brought great advantages for Stratford and the East End.
And this will be achieved again by creating new opportunities for local residents and companies near Heathrow.
For our local communities alone, we will create 10,000 new apprenticeships, 40,000 new jobs and put an end to youth unemployment in the airport environment.
Just as they were ahead of us in London 2012, there will be those who will think the challenge of Heathrow's expansion is too great.
I've been told that England will never be ready for the games and that we're going to embarrass ourselves on the world stage.
The doubters were wrong in 2012 and they're going to be wrong at Heathrow, too.
There are always obstacles to overcoming, but Heathrow answers every question asked to him.
Like the Olympics, Heathrow is an infrastructure project for many rather than individuals.
Expansion is one way to create up to 180,000 new jobs, £211bn of economic growth and leave a legacy of skills and employment both around the airport and across the UK.
We've had a great team at Heathrow that implemented a �£11bn-expensive infrastructure investment programme in time and budget, from the T5 - which was voted the world's best airport terminal for five years in a round, until the T2, which was built amid the world's two busiest catwalks.
Now they are ready to deliver a third catwalk, backed by £16 billion in private investment.
The 2012 London Games have been a huge success for GB and I'm confident that Heathrow's expansion will also be one.
It’s time for us to steer this Olympic spirit and broaden Heathrow, thereby securing our future trade, creating a legacy of skills and observation that England can be proud of.
Lord Paul Deighton is chairman of the Heathrow Board and former Commercial Secretary to the Treasury
Adblock Plus, Facebook in "cat and mouse game" regarding ad blocking
For companies that earn money with online advertising, according to one of the software manufacturers, attempts to circumvent ad blocking are equivalent to a "cat and mouse game".
On Thursday, just two days after Facebook announced that it would block ad blocking on its desktop page, Adblock Plus published a blog post in which it announced that users could update their filters in the program to re-block advertisements on the social network.
On Tuesday, Facebook announced that its desktop site would change encoding regarding its advertisements in order to make it more difficult to detect ad blockages about which part of the advertising page is.
But Adblock proclaimed that it found a new way to bypass the new codes.
In a blog post, Adblock Ben Williams' spokesman wrote that "this kind of back-and-forth battle between the display-blocking open source community and circumventants has been undercaded since the invention of ad blockers," and that it's possible for Facebook to come soon with a new solution.
The fact that the world's largest social media company has engaged in the fight is a strong indication of how worrying display blockers have become for the industry.
Worldwide, according to a study last year by Pagefair and Adobe, around 200 million people are using display blockers; nearly four times as many in 2013.
With 1.03 billion daily active users accessing Facebook via their mobile devices, mobile phone advertising accounts for 84 percent of Facebook’s advertising revenue.
The company is targeting ad blockers on desktop PCs, as mobile ad blockers have no effect on Facebook's mobile app for the social network and Instagram, a spokesperson said.
Advertising on desktop PCs accounts for a sufficiently large share of the revenue, sounght Facebook the investment of such a step seems worthwhile.
On Tuesday, Facebook announced that it would update its "ad preference tool" to give people more control over advertisements targeted.
On Thursday, Facebook claimed Adblock's new filters would be worrying because they'd also block content on the site that's not advertising.
"For the users, this does not represent a positive experience, so we plan to address the problem," a spokesperson for Facebook said in an e-mail statement.
Ad blockers are a blunt remedy, which is why we’ve instead focused on developer tools like ad preferences to put more control in people’s hands.
Woman run over during suspected suicide on Swiss highway
The victim was lying on the highway in the middle of the night.
Police call witnesses after a woman was run over in the early hours of Thursday in a suspected suicide of several vehicles on a highway.
The incident occurred on the A1 near Morges in the canton of Vaud shortly after midnight.
A woman laid down on the road in the dark "in an act of despair" and was hit by several cars, police in Vaud testified.
It is obvious that at the age of 49, who was run over by several vehicles, including a lorry, wanted to kill herself herself.
She died on the spot.
Several drivers have already been interviewed, but some have still not been identified, said the police, who as well as any witnesses to the incident.
An investigation has been opened, which is a "logical" step in such a situation, Gilles Hofstetter told Le Tribune de Geneve.
However, it remains to be seen whether one of the drivers will be indicated.
Was a clash preventable?
That's something the prosecutor has to decide," Hofstetter said.
Two similar cases over the past five years show that drivers aren't held accountable.
In each of the two cases - in which one person ran in the dark on the highway - it was decided motorists don't have to assume that a pedestrian walks on the highway in the middle of night, according to the newspaper.
Last year, a negligent homicide policewoman was acquitted after running over and killing a man who laid one night on the A1 near Payerne in August 2014.
The 22-year-old victim had been drunk and fell unconscious on the street, the court heard.
The court found that the policewoman -- who had responded to an emergency call -- drove in a justifiable manner and that the presence of a man in the street was a "perfectly extraordinary and unpredictable situation," the judge said.
Anyone who has further information on Thursday's incident near Morges should report to police on 021 644 83 46 or 021 644 44 44.
NASA climate model: Venus was once habitable
Venus, according to the projections of a new NASA climate model, was probably once habitable.
Scientists at NASA's Goddard Institute for Space Studies developed a model to discover Venus' past using tools and algorithms similar to those of climate scientists.
The model was designed to determine whether Venus, with its hot, waterless surface and CO2-dense atmosphere, once had Earth-like conditions.
Scientists have previously hinted that the slow revolution of Venus could be explained by its dense atmosphere.
But the latest simulsification suggests that Venus may have had a thin, Earth-like atmosphere and is still slowly continuing.
The model also shed light on the topography of early Venus.
The planet presumably had more dry areas than the Earth, but once had enough water and geographical variability to host lives and protected against rapid evaporation.
Early Venus also benefited from a sun that was 30 percent matter.
"In the GISS model sympathy, the slow rotation of Venus exposes its daily side to the sun for nearly two months," GISS scientist Anthony Del Genio explained in a press release.
This warms the surface and produces rain that creates a thick layer of clouds, which acts like an umbrella to shield the surface from much of the sun's heat.
The result is average temperatures, which are a few degrees cooler than those of the Earth today.
The model spawned a history of Venus that resembles evolution previously suspected by planetary scientists.
Even though it was presumably habitable once, Venus—over the course of several billions—was exposed to too much heat and ultra-violet light.
Their oceans evaporated and their water vapour was burned away by ultraviolet light.
As their hydrogen escaped, only a thick atmosphere of carbon dioxide with a deadly greenhouse effect was left - the Venus we know today.
The researchers shared the results of their new Venus climate model in the Geophysical Research Letters.
Patrick Roy resigns as Avalanche's coach
Patrick Roy resigned on Thursday as coach and vice-president of hockey operations of the Colorado Avalanche and led too little co-determination in the team’s decision-making process.
Alternative president/general manager of Colorado's Joe Sakic confirmed this decision, noting that the club will immediately look for a new coach.
"Over the past three years, I've fulfilled my duties as head coach and Vice President Of Hockey Operations of the Colorado Avalanche with energy, passion, and definience," the 50-year-old Roy said in a statement.
I've been thinking long and a lot over the course of the summer about how I could improve this team to give it more deepness that it needs and take it to a higher level.
To achieve this, the vision of coach and vice president of hockey operations must be perfectly matched to those of the organization.
He must also have a co-determination right in decisions affecting the team's performance.
These conditions are not currently being met.
Sakic, who hired Roy on May 23, 2013, thanked him for his contributions to the team.
"Patrick informed me about that decision today," Sakic said.
We all appreciate what he has done for our organization and wish him only the best for the future.
Roy was named the league's best coach during his debut season at the 2013-14 Avalanches.
He led the club to 112 points and to first place in Central Vision, but Colorado was beaten in the first round of Minnesota playoffs and missed the post-season in each of the last two years.
The Avalanches recorded a 39-39-4 mark in the 2015-16 season and fell five points behind the Wild for a second wildcard spot in the Western Conference.
As Hall of Fame goalkeeper, Roy won four Stanley Cups -- two pieces with the Montreal Canadiens and the Avalanche.
Norsea oil workers resume talks, suspend strike
The labour disputes regarding remuneration for North Sea oil workers are suspended, while all parties to the dispute sit down for a new round of talks, negotiators said.
Strikes against oilfield service provider Wood Group were carried out sporadically on platforms in the North Sea operated by Royal Dutch Shell.
"Wood Group as well as the Unite and RMT unions can confirm that we have agreed to restart the settlement process of the current dispute," it said in a joint statement.
We're going to hold some all-day sessions over the next few weeks, which will start early next year to address the issues anew.
The unions said their last strike, which is the first in over 25 years, was a protest against possible wage cuts of up to 30 per cent.
Wood Group this year has cut rates to its contractors in the North Sea by around 9 percent due to negative market pressures.
The company's installment cut follows a 10-percent decline for onshore contractors in 2014.
The company said that additional cuts give the company the space needed to get through the market downturn.
Shell has sacked thousands of people and announced plans to give up its oil and gas operations in up to 10 countries as it streamlines its operations as a result of its mega-merger with the British energy company BG Group.
Energy companies are under pressure due to lower crude oil prices, an economic stage that had intensified by the decision by the Britons in June to leave the European Union.
Scottish Parliament member Dave Anderson met with labor leaders earlier this week to discuss one of the biggest labor disputes that took place in the North Sea for a generation.
The North Sea is important to the regional economy, he said.
"In the North Sea and across the country, we need an economy that works for many and not just for a few," he said in a statement.
Less than $1.4 billion in spending on new projects in the North Sea is expected in 2016 compared with an average of around $7 billion over the past five years.
Kepler measures rotation rate of stars in Pleiades' star cluster
The latest mission with the Kepler space telescope has helped astronomers collect the most complete catalog of rotation rates under the stars of the Pleiades star cluster.
The new data helps astronomers to understand why stars in the star cluster rotate at different rates, as well as in what context the rotation periods are with planet formation.
"We hope that by comparing our results with other star clusters, we're learning more about the relationship between the mass of a star, its age, and even the history of its solar system," Luisa Rebull, a researcher at Caltech's Infrared Processing and Analysis Center in Pasadena, said in a press release.
The stellar heap of the Pleiades is 445 light-years from Earth.
Many stars in this star cluster, which formed around 125 million years ago, are now reaching adulthood.
They're turning the fastest now.
These ripening stars emit large amounts of stellar winds at peak rotation rates.
Since these winds travel through the magnetic fields of the stars, they have a braking effect on the stars.
When a star ages, its rate of rotation begins to slow.
Kepler measures the rotation rate of each star by recognizing the time between the appearance of a recognizable starry spot -- like sunspots.
The latest catalogue of rotation rates indicates that more massive stars rotate more slowly than less massive stars.
Rebull compares the stars to dancers, with larger stars spinning more slowly pirouettes than smaller ones.
"In the "ballet" of pleiades, we see that slow rotors tend to be more massive, whereas faster rotors tend to be very bright stars," Rebull said.
Since the Pleiaden's star cluster is so close, it serves as an ideal cosmic laboratory to compare stellary qualities such as rotation rates.
"The Pleiade's star cluster offers an anchor for theoretical models of stellar rotation in both directions, younger and older ones," Rebull said.
There is still a lot in front of us about which we would like to learn more about how, when and why stars are slowing down their rotation rates and, so to speak, hanging their "dance shoes".
Rebull and her colleagues have reported extensively in three newspaper reports on their latest analysis of Pleiaden's rotation rates, which will soon appear in the Astronomical Journal.
A controversial baseball star kicks off
Tears ran faster at Alex Rodriguez than U.S. baseball star in best days from base to base.
After his emotional resignation announcement, the 41-year-old professional will wear the jersey of his New York Yankees one last time on August 12 against the Tampa Bay Rays.
“I love this game and this team.”
"I'm saying 'Goodbye' to both of them," the 14-time all-star game attendee of Major League Baseball (MLB) explained.
Rodriguez was voted the league's most valuable player three times, winning the World Series with the Yankees in 2009.
His signature under a ten-year contract gave him 27 million US dollars annually since 2007.
Until the end of the contract on 31 December 2017, he will in future act as a consultant and youth coach - with a full salary.
As a player, Rodriguez repaided for a long time with performance.
With 696 home runs, 3114 hits and 2021 points for his teams ("Runs") for the Seattle Mariners (1994 to 2000), the Texas Rangers (2001 to 2004) and since 2005 the Yankees, he managed some milestones.
With Hall-of-Fame members Hank Aaron and Willie Mays, only two other baseball players have similar scores in key statistics.
But the 41-year-old should not be included in the fame hall of baseball.
Because as successful Rodriguez - nickname "A-Rod" - was on the field, so controversial is his demeanor outside the pitch.
As early as 2009, the superstar on "ESPN" admitted to eating banned steroids between 2001 and 2003.
In August 2013, it was suspended for 211 matches until the end of 2014 due to involvement in the so-called biogenesis scandal.
Rodriguez is considered a prime suspective to have received performance-enhancing funds for years through an anti-aging clinic in South Florida.
After a legal dispute, the ban was still reduced to 162 encounters.
"The league may even have done me a favor for 2014, because I played through 20 years without a break," Rodriguez said suffisantly at the time.
His farewell days are not silent either.
While many baseball legends get the big stage once again in their last games, Rodriguez frequently had to sit on the bench.
"My job doesn't involve a farewell tour."
"My job involves trying to win every game," Yankees coach Joe Girardi etched.
After all, Rodriguez, who was married to actress Cameron Diaz and is said to have the relationship with pop star Madonna and the actress Kate Hudson, received sympathy expressions from the fans of Yankee archrival Boston Red Sox.
"We want A-Rod," the supporters chanted against the Rodriguez team in the 4:9 defeat.
2016 Olympics: Chen Xinyi, Silwia Danekowa and Adrian Zielinski are first doping cases
Caught: Because of a positive doping test, weightlifter Adrian Zielinski is not allowed to start from Poland in Rio.
Rio de Janeiro - The 2016 Olympics are overshadowed by the first official doping cases.
One caught athlete almost won a medal.
Ahead of the seventh day of competition, the first official doping cases have become known to the Rio de Janeiro Olympic Games.
Chinese swimmer Chen Xinyi, 3000-metre obstacle runner Silwia Danekowa of Bulgaria and Polish weightlifter Adrian Zielinski tested positive in Brazil and expelled - immediately before the start of the already critically eyed athletics competitions, this news comes untimely.
Chen had already been diagnosed last Sunday's diuretic hydrochlorothiazide, which is used as a masking agent for doping substances.
The International Court of Sport CAS upheld its exclusion from the Olympics on Friday afternoon local time.
On Sunday, the 18-year-old had finished fourth in the final over 100 m butterfly, only by 0.09 seconds she missed the bronze medal.
On Friday, Chen should have taken part in the preliminaries over 50 m freestyle.
Chen has applied to the International Olympic Committee (IOC) for the opening of the B-sample and a hearing.
"The national association takes the matter "very seriously and demands full cooperation from Chen in the investigations," Xinhua said in a statement: "We will cooperate with the International Court of Sport CAS and will accept the verdict."
For the first time in history, it is no longer the IOC, but the CAS responsible for the sanctioning of the athletes with positive cases.
Also on the night of Rio, the news about the positive rehearsal at Danekowa leaked, the 33-year-old went on the offensive herself.
"I've had three tests, all negative, and my fourth test was positive," this was "an unbelievable shock," she told BNT television.
She was innocent, "the only explanation could be dietary supplements," the athlete explained.
According to the report, the banned substance is said to be the blood doping agent EPO.
Danekowa has been provisionally suspended, according to BNT, the B-sample is also said to have been positive.
Bulgaria's Prime Minister Boyko Borissov immediately came forward with criticism of the athlete.
"I don't understand why the athletes don't draw the right conclusions," he said: "We Bulgarians are under surveillance, so many have already been ruled out."
For example, all weightlifters from the chronically doping-burdened nation are not allowed to participate in Rio.
On Friday, the "Adrian Zielinski" case was also known, the London Olympic champion in the class up to 85 kg, like his brother Tomasz, tested positive for the anabolic steroid nandrolone three days earlier.
Both deny the intake, but both are not allowed to start in the class up to 94 kg.
Before the start of the track and field athletes, the Danekova case in particular is another serious blow for the reputation of the entire sport.
The world governing body IAAF had prohibited the entire Russian team, with one exception due to systematic doping, the runner nation Kenya also produces numerous bad news in Rio.
In swimming, the previous offenses in China had also caused violent clashes between the athletes.
This is harshly attacked Frenchman Camille Lahour at Chinese Olympic champion Sun Yang.
"Sun Yang pees purple."
"When I see the podium over 200 m free style, I want to vomit," Lacourt said on French radio.
Sun was also at the center of an altercation with Australian Mack Horton.
"I don't have time or respect for doping scammers," he said.
Sun had tested positive for stimulant trimetazidine at the Chinese championships in 2014, but only had to serve a three-month ban.
Data theft: So perfidious hackers extort their ransom
Programmers of extortion malware are currently specifically attacking companies - and are successful in doing so.
According to a recent survey by the malware defense specialists Malwarebytes among 540 companies in North America, Great Britain and Germany, 40 percent of the companies surveyed have been victims of an attack with blackmailer software at least once in the past twelve months.
In Germany, almost 20 percent of the companies were already the target of ransomware extortion.
40 percent of the victims willingly paid the ransoms demanded by the perpetrators in order to be able to use the attacked systems and data again.
The perpetrators specialize their blackmailer software according to the observations of malware bytes on attacks in corporate networks.
If they are successful there, the potentially extortion ransom is significantly higher than for private users.
"We are currently observing a frighteningly rapid evolution of the malware," Malwarebytes CEO Marcin Kleczynski explains in an interview with Welt.
In 2008, most malware programs were still designed to send advertisements.
Then the perpetrators discovered extortion software for themselves.
Private users were the target at first, but the switch to company attacks took place frighteningly quickly in recent months.
Ransomware works according to a simple pattern: Once active on a victim’s computer, she specifically searches for recently changed files, such as Word, Excel or database files, as well as archives of images and videos.
These files encrypt the ransomware and permanently delete the originals.
Subsequently, the software presents the demands of the blackmailers.
In order to get the end key code to the files, the victims have to transfer money - usually in the form of the crypto currency Bitcoin.
But where the personal photo database is at stake for private users, companies are concerned with sensitive customer data or project databases, without which the production stands still.
Accordingly, the damage is higher if a ransomware strikes.
22 percent of the affected German companies reported revenue losses due to ransomware, 13 percent even had to completely interrupt their business activities, in one case people were in danger of their lives.
In Germany, hospitals have been victims of attacks on several occasions whose patients can be acutely threatened by the loss of medical records.
"We always tell the victims: Don't pay, you only promote the problem with it," Kleczynski explains.
But 80 to 90 percent of the companies that pay get their data again.
It is difficult for managers to reject the demand of the perpetrators in view of a standstill in operation.
The hackers, in turn, know this and try everything to build a good reputation.
In a recent study, F-Secure’s security researchers examined the user interfaces and contact with the blackmailers and come to the conclusion that the perpetrators are actually trying to build up a kind of customer service in order to raise more money.
They tell their victims via chat how they can pay via Bitcoin, set up freely accessible support websites or decrypt sample files for free.
And if private individuals are redeemed after payments of a few hundred euros, companies sometimes have to negotiate hard and pay five-figure sums to get their data back unencrypted.
In view of the potentially high and relatively risk-free profits from ransomware extortion, the hackers are becoming more and more professional and sharing the work, explains security expert Chester Wisniewski of the antivirus provider Sophos: "A single person can write a ransomware approximately within a week."
However, it is more likely to be three or four people.
The programmers then sell their malware in the form of a software kit to the actual blackmailers.
The previous malware kits were created by buying the malware itself, advice and additional tools for a one-time, fixed price between 1000 and 10,000 dollars.
"The scope of delivery includes a customization tool, so that smaller configurations, appearance and branding can be changed," says Wisniewski.
The extortionists then look for more hackers to spread the freshly purchased software among the victims.
We live in a service economy.
So you log into one of the forums underground, buy the set from a criminal and instruct another to install the software on as many PCs as possible.
The payment is made per infected computer.
"Usually, the criminals then commission spammers to spread," Wisniewski explains.
The risk of ransomware is now known by many companies that better secure their data.
More than 50 percent of corporate IT managers in the Malwarebytes survey rely on being able to fend off ransomware using file back-ups and scanner software.
The perpetrators are already pursuing new strategies.
"If the encryption of data alone is no longer a sufficient threat to successful shuttering, the perpetrators threaten to publish sensitive data such as customer lists or price calculations instead," explains Malwarebytes CEO Marcin Kleczynski.
Depending on the nature of the data, the damage potential is thus many times higher than before.
The perpetrators could also target completely new goals in the Internet of Things in the future.
At the Defcon hacker conferencing conference, security researchers demonstrated how they were able to get a thermostat connected to the Internet under their control.
In the middle of winter, the activation of such a blocked heater could well be worth a ransom for many victims.
If the perpetrators then block an entire production line in a factory instead of heating in the future, ransomware could also become an issue in Industry 4.0.
One in all cases
HONDA HR-V is flexible and suitable for everyday use
Honda wants to pack everything in the small HR-V: a little SUV, a little coupé and also the versatility of a minivan.
Sounds quite cluttered, but the HR-V, which has been constructed on the same new platform as the new jazz, actually proves to be extremely flexible in everyday life.
Although the HR-V, which is only 1.60 m high, seems rather small, the passengers have a lot of space both in the front row and in the rear, it does not seem narrow at any point.
The materials of the interior are well-made and look pleasant, only the cockpit top falls off.
The HR-V is more versatile than comparable vehicles when it comes to storage space.
There is not only a trunk space of 453 l at raised and 1026 l for folded backrests, but there are the "Magic Seats".
That's less magical than it sounds: The seats of the rear seat can be folded up like cinema chairs, thus providing storage space that's 1.24 m high from the floor to the ceilings.
The trunk is well loaded, but the loading height of 650 mm is slightly higher than the comparable Peugeot 2008.
Thanks to the torque of up to 300 Nm, the tested 1.6-l diesel with 88 kW/120 hp accelerates the HR-V very powerfully in wide speed ranges thanks to its torque.
However, he is likely to do his work a little quieter.
The everyday consumption of 4.7 l/100 km is pleasing.
There is another engine: a 1.5-l petrol engine with 96 kW/130 hp.
An all-wheel drive is not offered.
The driving behavior is balanced, a good compromise between dynamism and comfort.
However, the steering is likely to be a bit more direct.
The equipment leaves nothing to be desired in the tested top variant "Executive".
Safety systems include the City emergency braking assistant (works up to tempo 32), the collision warning system and the lane keeping assistant.
The collision warner sometimes irritates, because he assesses many obstacles as "approaching objects".
The high-beam assistant works very reliably.
And the intelligent speed controller responds to traffic sign recognition and throttles the speed if necessary.
However, full connectivity is only available for the top equipment: 7-inch touchscreen, navigation, app integration and more.
Two USB jacks and one HDMI jack are under the armrest and can therefore be reached a little awkwardly.
The approximately 29000 euros for the well-equipped top diesel are fine.
If you don't want to invest so much, you get the petrol engine from 20000 euros, the diesel from 23000 euros.
The engines meet the Euro 6 emission standard.
With the HR-V, Honda offers a versatile car suitable for everyday use.
Airline is not liable: Fall on passenger bridge
An airline is not liable for the fall of a passenger on the passenger bridge.
Liability is only conceivable in the case of hazards that are typical for air traffic, argued the Higher Regional Court of Düsseldorf (Az.: 18 U 124/14).
The plaintiff had broken a kneecap in the fall.
However, the accident could have happened anywhere - it was a general risk of life, according to the court.
The airline didn't have to pay the man any damages.
Sickness case: When does the organizer pay
Affected holidaymakers only get money back after their trip if the cause of the disease can undoubtedly be found in the hotel.
However, this is only the case if more than ten percent of the guests are proven to show the same symptoms.
In these cases, a familia was seriously ill with diarrhea and vomiting on the second day of their Turkey holiday.
She spent almost the entire holiday in the room.
Her illness led the holidaymakers back to a faulty sewage treatment plant that had supposedly polluted the seawater.
The family was of the opinion that the trip had been deficient - and complained against the organizer.
The plaintiffs were unsuccessful in court.
In fact, the sewage treatment plant was defective.
But the organizer only learned about it shortly before departure of the plaintiffs.
He had to assume until then that the seawater was fine.
He did not violate his information obligations, according to the verdict.
Especially since no impurities were found in the samples of the water
The illness of several hotel guests - possibly due to contagion with each other - belongs to the general risk of life.
Out of 691 guests, only 38 were ill.
De Maizière said that after the violence committed by refugees in Würzburg and Ansbach in July, many people were worried about further attacks.
The state must respondly and resolutely at the same time to
What is certain is that our country will not answer the violence of the perpetrators with hatred and division.
We won't give this triumph to terrorists.
If offenders had to leave the country more quickly, however, this would increase security and also strengthen the consent of the population for refugee policy.
De Maizière intends to achieve the implementation of the proposals by the 2017 federal election.
He also announced a further personnel reinforcement of the security forces, "on a medium four-figure scale over several years."
Sympathy advertising for terrorism must again be punished.
Video surveillance is to be improved, the gun right should be amended in such a way that the purchase of guns on the Internet in particular is made more difficult.
The recently much-discussed duty of secrecy for doctors is to be safeguarded, according to de Maizière's words.
However, he wants to look in dialogue with the medical profession for solutions on how the endangerment of the citizens can be reduced, he said with regard to psychological abnormalities.
In order to counteract a radicalization of refugees, the Minister considers better social care to be necessary.
Teachers should be better prepared to deal with traumatized people.
De Maizière plans the use of covert investigators to prosecute criminals in special areas of the Internet (Darknet).
SPD leader Gabriel said: "The SPD is ready to talk about everything that helps to further increase security".
It is important that the Minister of the Interior has clearly spoken out against actionism.
"This was a slap in the face for the sharpeners in the CDU/CSU," Gabriel told the newspapers of Funke Mediengruppe (Friday).
De Maizière had previously rejected calls from the Union for a burqa ban and the abolition of dual nationality.
He considers a general prohibition of full veiling to be constitutionally problematic.
You can't forbid everything you refuse, and I'm refusing to wear the burqa.
You can talk about rules, for example in road traffic or for witnesses in court.
Greens leader Simone Peter accused the minister of wanting to simulate energy and ability to act with new anti-terrorism packages.
Bernd Riexinger said: "Fleet sayings and a tightening of the right of residence do not bring more security to the people in this country".
Better traffic flow on the motorway feeder
It regularly jammes on the bypass road - here's a picture from November.
But this is to change: The right-turn lane is to be extended.
This is good news for all motorists who are stuck in a traffic jam every night when driving over the motorway shuttle (A 43) in professional traffic.
Next Tuesday and Wednesday (16/17 August) in the area between Hammer Straße and Weseler Straße, the Landesbetrieb Straßenbau will extend the right-turning lane by 700 metres.
Drivers can then thread directly from Hammer Straße to the right.
The big advantage is that the traffic is divided earlier, which improves the overall procedure on the busy section of Bundesstraße 51.
The CDU faction in the Münster-West district representation came up with the idea for the extended exit.
The constriction of the road on the feeder annoys motorists especially between 5 p.m. and 7 p.m.
On weekdays, the backwater extends to the bridge over the Albersloher Weg and sometimes beyond.
The reason is the eye on the B 51 shortly before the Weseler Straße exit.
From here, the traffic is steered over the motorway bridge only one-lane.
The second track had to be dismantled a good three-quarters of years ago.
The reason: Static calculations had shown that the bridge no longer has the required load-bearing capacity in order, above all, to accommodate heavy-duty traffic in two lanes.
How long this bottleneck and the associated weekday traffic jam will last, the responsible state company cannot say.
However, years are to be expected: because the bridge has an official remaining useful period until the year 2034.
For the extension of the right-turning lane, the state company has deliberately selected the lower-traffic time during the school holidays, because disabilities are to be expected.
The traffic will be routed one-lane past the construction site on Tuesday and Wednesday.
Intercultural travel: cultural offerings for schools
The international class of the Waldschule attended the play "The Invisible House".
Nima's association and the Junges Theater are now planning a music project.
The Nima's association enables young people with a migrant and refugee background to visit cultural institutions in Münster.
The "Class International" of the Waldschule Kinderhaus has attended a performance of the award-winning play "The Invisible House" of the Junges Theater Münster.
In the context of the "intercultural journey" initiated by the association Nima's, 19 students between the ages of eleven and 15 visited the staging in the small house.
Since the beginning of the year, the association, which has set itself the goal of the cultural integration of young people with a migrant and refugee background, offers schools with welcome classes the opportunity to visit cultural institutions in Münster as part of this project.
Most young people in the class came to Münster between September 2015 and March 2016 from Afghanistan, Iran, Serbia and Syria, among others, and spoke little German.
Thanks to the choreographies, the scenic images and video animations, they were still able to follow the course of action, according to the press release of the Junges Theater Münster.
The association Nima's, founded by Dr. Tannaz Westerberg from Sprakel, and the Young Theater will work together as part of a musical theatre project in the coming season.
From October to December, young people with and without refugee and immigrant background between the ages of 14 and 20 will be given the opportunity to attend professionally guided courses in the fields of singing, dancing, acting and music.
Offers for children and families in the area of theatre play and theatrical language teaching are planned.
The "intercultural journey" project continues.
In view of the increased number of refugees, the association expects many classes that want to participate.
Rain instead of shooting stars: Hope for weather improvement
The weather has caused the shooting stars in the south-west of Germany in the night to Friday a dash - but there is still hope.
The night was badly suitable to observe the celestial spectacle because of clouds and rain, the chairman of Welzheim observatory, Hans-Ulrich Keller, said on Friday morning.
Our hopes are on Saturday night.
The weather is supposed to be good, then I'm sure we'll see a lot of shooting stars in the sky.
The star scales are Perseids, which are among the fastest shooting stars with 216000 kilometers per hour.
They have their name because they seem to come from the constellation of Perseus, which rises in the northeast.
Relaxing Night: Tips for Sleep Disturbances
In the evening you roll around forever, at night you lie awake for hours, in the morning you can no longer think about sleep far before the alarm ringing.
That's anything but relaxing.
After all, one is in good company with such problems: according to the Robert Koch Institute, about a quarter of adults have sleep disorders and more than ten percent perceive their sleep frequently or permanently as unrelastic.
How do sleep problems or sleeping disorders manifest themselves?
There are several forms of sleep disorders, and they can underline a variety of causes, emphasizes Hans-Günter Weeß, member of the board of directors of the German Society for sleep research and sleep medicine.
What is commonly understood, however, is insomnia, i.e. an insomnia and through-sleep disorder.
In particular, many people find it unsatisfactory or insufficient, says Jens Wagenknecht, a board member of the German GPs Association with practice in Varel, Lower Saxony.
What’s behind sleep disorders?
The causes of sleep disorders are complex.
There may be organic, psychic or behavioral factors, but also drugs.
It is not uncommon that the sleep disorder is an expression of another disease.
"Very early waking up is common, especially with depression," Weeß says.
And it may be that different factors depend on each other.
For example, if you have a herniated disc, you can lie badly, wake up every two to three hours and you might start pondering about everyday problems and worries, Weeß gives an example.
This leads to tension, and sleep is no longer possible.
From when should you go to the doctor with sleep disturbances?
Those who do not sleep well over a period of one month in at least three nights per week and are impaired during the day should go to the doctor, Weeß advises.
The impairment can look very different: you are knocked off, tired, unslept, have difficulties with attention and concentration, is easily irritable, has mood swings or physical complaints such as stomach or headaches.
In order to alleviate the sleep disorder, it is important to find out their causes.
Treatment options include behavioral changes, cognitive behavioral therapy or also medications - classic sleeping pills only in exceptional cases and at most for two weeks.
There are hardly any resident sleep physicians in Germany, emphasizes Prof. Ingo Fietze, head of the interdisciplinary sleep medical center of Charité Universitätsmedizin Berlin.
That’s why you should first contact the family doctor in case of sleep problems, Weeß advises.
He can clarify with examinations whether the problems have a physical cause.
Who should go to the sleep lab, and what's going to happen?
For the examination in the sleep laboratory, doctors recommend, for example, in snoring with respiratory lapses, in the case of morbid daytime fatigue, in sleepwalkers or if one does not find the cause of a sleep-through disorder, explains Fietze.
You don’t really have to prepare for the investigation.
One should spend the day normally.
"Just please don't take a nap," Fietze says.
After wiring you go to bed at your normal time.
In addition to the electrodes on the body, you wear nasal glasses, a chest strap and a feeler on the finger.
In addition, a camera on the ceiling records the sleeping person.
How can those affected get to grips with the problems?
There are quite a lot of adjusting screws that you can put on for better sleep.
Weeß enumerates: Regular sleeping and getting-up times, not lying in bed for too long, not sleeping during the day, not watching TV to fall asleep, avoiding alcohol and not looking at the clock at night - then you immediately begin to reckon, and that's not conducive at all.
What the jungle camp has to do with missing medals in swimming
Former German swimmer Markus Deibler has commented via Facebook on the disappointing performance of the DSV team at Olympis.
In December 2014, he won gold for the first time at the Short Course World Championships in Doha and set his first world record at the same time.
Now the former DSV athlete Markus Deibler has placed himself in front of his criticized ex-teammates via Facebook.
"In a country where an Olympic champion gets €20,000 and €150,000 a jungle king shouldn't surprise anyone about missing medals," the 26-year-old wrote on his Facebook page on the night.
The support system in swimming sports is poor, but the doping controls are very good.
With this, we cannot compete with countries that promote a great deal and do not look so closely on the subject of doping or even operate it.
"I'm not saying the checks should be stopped here," he further explained.
If he wanted to start a comeback, Deibler would have to be reported nine months before his first launch by the National Anti-Doping Agency Germany (NADA).
That's exactly how you should keep it with countries, too.
"Anyone who cannot be controlled by independent controllers of WADA/FINA for a certain time before World Cup/OS may not participate," urges Wahl-Hamburger, who now operates an ice cream parlor.
Airport: Gang of baggage thieves arrested
Professional thieves at Düsseldorf Airport
Civil investigators of the Düsseldorf police succeeded on Thursday the arrest of two women and two men aged 50 to 61.
The suspects had committed as a gang several bag and baggage thefts.
At about 21:30, civilian officials in the area of Düsseldorf Airport recognized a gang, which was known to the policemen from past theft offenses.
The two women and two men had committed several offences since mid-July this year and had proceeded jointly, work-sharing and particularly professionally.
The specialists of the Crime Commission 34 can prove four acts to the thieves, who had entered Germany specifically to commit crimes.
They had captured a total of over 15,000 euros.
Old buildings, a few linden trees, in between still a remnant cobblestones: Rigaer Straße in Berlin-Friedrichshain does not look like this at first glance, but the police count it among the "dangerous places" of the city.
If you walk along the street from east to west, you pass playgrounds, a freshly painted kindergarten and an organic shop.
On the façade of a gray house, someone sprayed "ugly!"
Opposite a Lidl, which will soon be demolished, it is already being built: By 2017, a house with high windows and 133 new rental apartments is to be built here.
However, articles about this "trendy district", as the responsible real estate company Friedrichshain, are preferably illustrated with burning cars in recent weeks.
The reason is the house with the number 94: It was once occupied, meanwhile the residents have leases, but on the ground floor there are still rooms and are used for events.
Since the beginning of the year, the Berlin interior senator Henken in particular had repeatedly advocated a crackdown by the police against the project, describing the area as a "no-go zone" for police officers and 94 as the starting point of autonomous violence.
After vacating rooms on the ground floor on June 22, with 300 police officers, police patrolled for three weeks with 30 to 40 officers and three crew cars on the site, blocking parts of the street and stopping visitors from entering the house with the number 94.
Meanwhile, on behalf of the unknown homeowners, construction workers renovated the rooms.
On July 13, a Berlin administrative court ruled that the eviction had been illegal, and a day later, police and construction workers withdrew under the cheers of sympathizers and local residents.
On this summer afternoon, the street seems peaceful: parents walk with children, people sit on the sidewalk in front of the houses in the sun, others converse in front of Späti.
In the immediate vicinity of the house projects are a primary school and kindergartens.
Left-wing violence is not the main concern of many local residents: Kerstin Neugebauer, who has lived in the neighborhood since 1999, and Jana*, who raises three children here, are especially annoyed by the police presence.
Together with the residents of the house projects, they share concerns about rising rents and gentrification.
It doesn't sound like a neighborhood, the majority wanted police protection from vandalling leftists.
The trench that the Berlin interior senator and the police open between squatters and their neighbors apparently does not exist.
Friedrichshain in the east of Berlin was actually a traditional working-class district, which still has a low average income and a high risk of poverty compared to the rest of the city.
Occupations and the resulting housing projects have also been part of the neighbourhood for a long time: in 1990, numerous houses in the former GDR were occupied, including almost the entire Mainzer Straße very close to Rigas.
While the people of Mainz had been vacated in a veritable street battle under a large police force, many of the projects were legalized in the Rigas and are partly still inhabited today by the people who occupied them 26 years ago.
At the same time, the district has increasingly become a magnet for tourists and election Berliners from all over the world in recent years: residents from GDR times and former squatters now share their neighbourhood with them.
This is also noticeable in rents, which have risen by almost 60 per centures for new rentals since 2009.
Many of the new buildings in the Riga are attacked as symbols for this development: they can be recognized by throw-in panes or freshly painted façades that have been pelted with paint bags.
No other street has been mentioned so often this year along with the words "left-autonom
One reason why not all neighbors see this as a problem could be that they are not necessarily affected by crime: according to crimes such as bodily harm, according to statistics, almost exclusively against policians.
The majority of the offenses are property damage, which tends to affect the new buildings and newly renovated houses.
Kerstin Neugebauer, who has lived in a tenement building next to the controversial 94 for 17 years, there can be no talk of uncertainty.
As far as acts of violence are concerned, she is appeasing: the people in 94 are her neighbors, they were certainly not.
Even the famous burning cars have not existed directly in the Rigas for a long time.
For the studied architect, her street is not a criminal focal point, on the contrary: "Here you help each other, live together on the street and get more from each other than elsewhere".
Neugebauer has deliberately moved into Rigaer Straße - and remained.
That's why it was so important to her that the local residents get their streets back: from the police, the politicians and the media, who in July all suddenly claimed the sovereignty of interpretation over the district.
But in the eyes of residents such as Neugebauer, the road is still taken away from them: through faceless new buildings in former construction gaps, which displace the old inhabitants.
"These are ultimately condos, which are not interesting for many here - there are people who aren't so well-heeled," she says.
"Violence and destruction I absolutely cannot approve."
But what are you supposed to do if no one ever listens?
Frustration begins on all sides, even with the police and the residents.
Kerstin Neugebauer has little understanding for immigrants who are annoyed by color bombs: the house projects and the left-wing scene belonged to the neighborhood.
Those who move here should be aware - just like someone who moves to a pubic district can complain poorly about noise there.
