Urbach: Annika Deuschle is the new refugee commissioner
Annika Deuschle is the point of contact in terms of integration and organizer.
She is also a social worker on site.
Annika Deuschle has been the Refugee Commissioner for Urbach since July.
The social educator supports the team of the administration around Achim Grockenberger.
She sees herself as a point of contact for all actors: Refugees, working group refugee aid, administration, authorities and who else is involved.
Regine Kunde from the working group suggests that the volunteers are more than grateful that Deuschle is now there, the egg-laying woolly milk sow, which above all has a problem.
For examples, some - less mocking than aimed at the large number of desired areas of responsibility - had meant when the administration wanted to advertise the position.
An administrative specialist with a talent for languages and people, combined with many years of experience in the field of social labour, was searched for an egg-laying woolly milk sow.
But administrative work is not their central area, says Annika Deuschle.
Above all, it is about organization, working with the people on the ground and also behind the scenes as well as networking activities.
The 31-year-old has beened for around six weeks.
It has limited a 50 per cent position to three years.
"It's been high time," says Regine Kunde that Annika Deuschle supports honorary and full-time actors.
There are major and small problems, organizational, human, and communication problems.
Soon there will be new containers in Wasenstraße
A serious problem has beened for some time to find apartments for the refugees who have been recognized.
The municipalities are responsible for follow-up accommodation.
You have to accommodate the refugees in your own or rented living spaces.
The costs for the construction, rent or renovation of these shelters must be borne by the municipalities themselves.
Responsibility changes from the district office to the municipality and job center.
Problem: There are free objects, but not every landlord wants to offer his apartment.
The search has been very difficult for some time now.
In fact, the administration finds no private apartments.
So far, it has not worked, although one is dependent on private housing, according to Deuschle.
Therefore, communal dwellings must be upgraded, as it is so beautifully called.
So new containers will soon be installed in Wasenstraße.
However, it is not done only with housing.
Perspective is the keyword, Regine Kunde and Annika Deuschle agree.
In addition to a permanent residence, there are internships, apprenticeships, school places, one-euro jobs and temporary activities for refugees and migrants that must be found or established.
"More seats would be good," reports Regine Kunde.
Many work in the construction yard or help caretakers, do charitable work - and this motivated and very tidy, as Annika Deuschle reports.
It hopes that the work for the community will lead to a positive external impact.
"It's not occupational therapy."
Activity doesn’t just mean work.
The day needs a fixed structure, Deuschle and customer say.
People should develop a sense of belonging.
So it needs offers such as sports, contacts with locals and especially language courses.
Professional courses are rare.
So here, too, administration and volunteers are stepping in.
It is central that the refugees get the feeling that they are wanted and welcome in Germany.
The long duration of the procedures is a problem.
They have to explain to the refugees that it just takes time and they can meaningfully bridge the time, for example with language courses.
Anyone who eventually develops the feeling that he cannot help anyone in Urbach, is not allowed to work and is useless, he will never integrate.
The clear majority is motivated.
But there are also problem cases, says customer honestly, "people who can't do anything". The work of Deuschle and customer is therefore always to motivate others.
All this is already a "mammoth task".
A difficult question: After the recent incidents, are the two afraid of radicalization, even in a small community such as Urbach?
"No," says Regine Kunde quite clearly, "what some may call naive," but a residual risk cannot and does not want to exclude them.
But the helpers are already sensitized.
These incidents are also an issue for refugees.
They're afraid they're afraid that the assassins will put them in disrepute completely.
"They want to show that they have nothing to do with it," says Regine Kunde.
Many would like to stay in Urbach.
Therefore, full-time and volunteers on the ground and in the region have to look for activities.
In doing so, they always have in the background that a new push of refugees and migrants could soon come to Urbach.
Does Germany need an upper limit?
Clearly answering this question, politics shundles.
Actually, it's unfair to put helpers on the ground.
"We haven't arrived in Urbach where we want to go yet," says Annika Deuschle.
No new refugees, that would relax the situation.
Regine Kunde also makes it clear that Urbachers are also used to sensibly integrate the previous people.
That's about 100 people at the moment, mostly young men.
But when people are in need, you have to help them, both women agree.
The political world situation is so complicated, there are no easy answers.
